y the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["sysadminsuite", "sysadmin suite", "Active Directory", "AD OU move", "computer OU", "organizational unit", "Move-ADObject", "directory administration", "batch OU move", "distinguished name", "windows administration"], "profile": "sysadminsuite", "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 1149, "discoveryGroup": "sequence", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P122", "seq": "122", "name": "Web Contact Form Semantic Validation & Mutation-Hardened Release Guard", "type": "BUILD + HARDEN", "class": "WEB / FORM SECURITY & VALIDATION", "sprintRole": "Harden web contact-form handling with semantic form-control association, computed-access defenses, payload and resource guards, CRLF-normalized mutation fixtures, and built-output release gating so injection and bypass defects are rejected deterministically", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "A web application exposes a contact or email form (for example Next.js/React with Resend and Turnstile) and validation must resist DOM semantic bypasses, external form association, computed request access, console leakage, payload forgery, second-sink execution, and HTML/comment/template escapes", "inspectFirst": "Current form markup including inside-form and external form=\"contact-form\" controls, unnamed controls and native submit elements, required-attribute ordering, all request-access surfaces (direct, computed, aliased), console usage, Resend payload construction, Turnstile resource inclusion (script and passive resource hints), existing validators/tests, mutation fixtures and their line-ending handling, built-output artifacts, package.json scripts and release:check composition, and the deployed HTML/template output", "expectedOutput": "Semantic form-control association enforced, unnamed/external native-submit rejected, required-attribute order-insensitive, computed/aliased request access blocked, all-console denied, Resend payload semantically validated, second-sink covered, CRLF-normalized fixtures green on Windows and Unix, HTML-aware tag boundaries plus comment-safe payload location plus template-interpolation rejection plus passive Turnstile-resource detection proven, and mutation plus built-output bound into release:check with provider proof", "nextStep": "Prototype the smallest CRLF-normalized fixture that falsifies the first escaping mutation (for example a comment-wrapped payload or external form control), harden the semantic validators, bind mutation and built-output checks into release:check, prove sensitivity and site parity, then iterate the next sink or boundary Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "Every semantic, payload, console, and resource check is deterministic and CRLF-stable; second-sink and tag/comment/template escapes are covered; mutation fixtures prove sensitivity on both line endings; built-output is validated from the same release:check; release:check fails closed on mutation, stale build, or drift; and generated-site/exact-head provider proof passes", "color": "Cyan", "copySheet": "P122_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "HARDEN THE WEB CONTACT FORM WITH SEMANTIC VALIDATION, MUTATION FIXTURES, AND BUILT-OUTPUT-GATED RELEASE. DO NOT SHIP A FORM THAT PASSES ONLY ON HAPPY-PATH INPUTS.\n\nRepo: xyz_repo_or_path\nForm route/handler: xyz_contact_form_route_or_resolve_from_repo\nStack: xyz_nextjs_react_resend_turnstile_or_resolve_from_repo\n\nMISSION\nMake the contact/email form reject bypasses that survive naive string checks. Treat HTML semantics, request-access indirection, console leakage, email-payload forgery, second-sink execution, and build-artifact drift as first-class failure modes. Start from current markup and handler evidence, harden validators with HTML-aware and semantic checks, cover each bypass with a CRLF-stable mutation fixture that can both pass and fail, and bind the whole guard into release:check so a green unit suite cannot hide a stale vulnerable build.\n\nWHERE TO LOOK FIRST\n- Form markup: every <input>, <textarea>, <select>, <button> inside <form id=\"contact-form\"> and every external control with form=\"contact-form\"; unnamed controls; native submit vs JS submit; required attribute position/order; disabled/hidden controls.\n- Request surfaces: direct req.body access, computed access (req[\"body\"], req[variable], destructured alias), aliased handler arguments, middleware-normalized bodies, and framework helpers that re-expose fields under new names.\n- Console surfaces: all console.* calls across app, api, handler, and client code.\n- Email payload: Resend (or equivalent) from/to/subject/text/html construction, template interpolation sites, and any concatenation of user fields into payload.\n- Turnstile/CAPTCHA: script/widget import, explicit render calls, token verification, and passive resource hints (preload/prefetch/preconnect, CSS/JS resource detection beyond active script execution).\n- Tests/fixtures: existing validators, mutation fixtures, built-output directory, and package.json scripts including release:check.\n- Built output: what release:check actually executes, whether it rebuilds then validates, and whether it consumes built HTML/JS.\n\n1. SEMANTIC FORM-CONTROL ASSOCIATION\nControls belong to a form by spec, not by proximity.\n- A control with form=\"contact-form\" is associated even when outside the <form> element. A control without form attribute and outside the form is not associated.\n- Resolve association by HTML semantics: ancestor form chain plus explicit form attribute, not string proximity or file order.\n- UseWhen entrypoints: internal text/email/message inputs, external form=\"contact-form\" honeypots or injected fields, and any form-associated custom element.\n- Mutations: add/remove an external form=\"contact-form\" control, move a control outside the form without form attribute, change form id without updating form attribute.\n\n2. UNNAMED AND EXTERNAL NATIVE-SUBMIT REJECTION\nUnnamed controls and external native submits are bypass vectors.\n- Reject any form-associated control that has no name attribute or has empty name, regardless of id/class/placeholder.\n- Reject external native submit: <button type=\"submit\" form=\"contact-form\"> or <input type=\"submit\" form=\"contact-form\"> that is not inside the intended form. Programmatic fetch/JS submit owned by the app is separate; native HTML submit that escapes client validation is the defect.\n- Required-attribute order handling: treat required as order-insensitive and value-insensitive (required, required=\"\", required=\"required\", required=\"true\" are all required). Do not match required by literal string order or exact required=\"required\" only.\n\n3. COMPUTED AND ALIASED REQUEST ACCESS DEFENSES\nAttackers and future refactors use indirection. The guard must too.\n- Deny computed access to request fields: req[\"body\"], req[dynamic], req.body[dynamic], (req as any)[field], and destructured aliases (const {body: b}=req). Normalize field access to a single validated extraction path.\n- Aliased handler parameters: function handler(reqAlias, res) where reqAlias is later used as reqAlias.body[\"field\"] must be tracked as the same sink.\n- Fixtures: one valid direct-access control that must pass, one computed-access bypass that must fail, one aliased-access bypass that must fail.\n\n4. ALL-CONSOLE DENIAL\nDo not allow console.* as a logging side-channel for form data or verification tokens.\n- Deny all console methods (log, info, warn, error, debug, trace, table) in form, api, handler, and adjacent validation code. No allowlist.\n- Fixtures: a console.log(userEmail) fixture that must be rejected, and a validated control without console that must pass.\n\n5. SEMANTIC RESEND PAYLOAD CHECKING\nDo not check Resend by string containing \"Resend\" alone.\n- Validate semantic payload shape: the Resend (or email provider) send/create call receives an object whose to/from/subject/text/html fields are not unsanitized concatenation of raw user fields. Use AST or semantic call-site inspection, not substring.\n- Reject template-interpolation injection: payload html/text that interpolates ${userInput}, {{userInput}}, {userInput}, or JSX {userInput} without escaping/sanitization. HTML-aware tag boundary: reject payloads that can close a tag or inject a new element because user input is placed inside tag content/attributes without escaping.\n- Comment-safe payload location remains a bypass: payload html that places user input inside <!-- userInput --> or other comment contexts must be rejected unless the output context proves comments are not executed. Location inside comments is not safe.\n- Fixture pair: one sanitized Resend payload that must pass, one interpolated-into-html payload and one comment-placed payload that must fail.\n\n6. SECOND-SINK MUTATIONS\nFirst-sink guard is necessary but not sufficient.\n- After initial validation/sanitization, track second-sink handlers: alternate api route, webhook forward, queue job, email text vs html dual sink, analytics sink, or log sink that re-uses the raw field.\n- Add at least one second-sink mutation where the first sink is sanitized but the second re-uses the raw user field and must be rejected.\n\n7. CRLF-NORMALIZED MUTATION FIXTURES\nWindows checkout line endings must not mask failures.\n- Normalize fixture input and expected output with CRLF handling: the same logical payload must assert identically under \\r\\n and \\n. Run fixtures or normalize line endings before comparison so a test that passes on Unix but fails on Windows is a defect, not a flaky environment.\n- Provide at least: one CRLF-normalized mutation fixture (CRLF and LF variants prove same verdict), one semantic form-control fixture (external form=\"contact-form\" included, outside-without-form excluded), one unnamed/external-submit rejection fixture, and one required-attribute-order-agnostic fixture.\n\n8. HTML-AWARE TAG BOUNDARIES, COMMENT-SAFE LOCATION, TEMPLATE-INTERPOLATION, AND PASSIVE TURNSTILE-RESOURCE DETECTION (0a7a4c4… HARDENING)\nThis is the HTML-aware boundary set.\n- Tag boundary: validate payload/resource injection with an HTML-aware parser or tag-boundary heuristic, not raw string contains. Reject user input that can terminate a tag or cross into a new tag/attribute context.\n- Comment-safe payload location: do not treat HTML comment context as safe. Validate payload placement against comment boundaries.\n- Template-interpolation rejection: reject any user field that flows into template interpolation sites (JS template literals, JSX expression interpolation, Mustache/Handlebars/Next template interpolation) without context-aware escaping. Literal concatenation and interpolated forms are both sinks.\n- Broader passive Turnstile-resource detection: detect Turnstile beyond active script execution: passive resource hints and resource-loaded verification. Examples: <link rel=\"preload\" href=\"...turnstile...\">, <link rel=\"preconnect\">, imported Turnstile/CAPTCHA resources detected as passive resources, not only active Turnstile widget script execution or inline turnstile.render(). Detecting only active script presence is a bypass.\n- Fixtures: one tag-boundary bypass, one comment-placed payload bypass, one template-interpolated payload bypass, and one passive Turnstile resource that must be detected.\n\n9. MUTATION + BUILT-OUTPUT BINDING INTO release:check\nA green mutation suite against stale source is not safety.\n- Define release:check (or the repo's canonical aggregated check) as: build -> validate source fixtures -> validate built-output fixtures -> any active resource/wiring check. Mutations must run against built output as well as source when the form/email payload is constructed from build artifacts or compiled templates.\n- Make mutation failures and built-output mismatches fail closed: non-zero exit, no silent skip when build is missing, no passing when built HTML still contains vulnerable interpolation/comment placement.\n- Provide exact commands that prove the binding: e.g., npm run build && npm run test:mutation && npm run test:built-output && npm run release:check, or the repo's actual script composition. Document the script composition in package.json so CI and local runs share the same gate.\n\n10. ITERATION AND PROOF\n- Second pass: after one green run, seed one wrong semantic association, one computed-access bypass, one console leak, one Resend-shaped but sanitized-missing payload, one second-sink reuse, one CRLF-only failure, one tag-boundary escape, one comment-safe misclassification, one template-interpolation miss, and one passive-Turnstile-resource miss. Each must be caught.\n- Preserve neighboring owners: P113 owns test-floor evolution mechanics; P92 owns canonical path resolution (never hide a Windows CRLF failure behind a path fix); P94 owns regression preservation. This prompt owns form semantic validation and mutation-hardened release gating; do not duplicate their mechanics inside this prompt.\n\nDELIVER\nReport: form association model used; unnamed/external-submit disposition; required-attribute handling; computed/aliased access verdict; console scan result; Resend payload verdict with sink count; second-sink coverage; CRLF-normalized mutation count and Windows/Unix parity evidence; tag/comment/template/Turnstile detection results; release:check composition and built-output proof; files changed; commit/PR/mainline SHA; proof ceiling; and one exact next command.\n\nCLOSURE RULE\nClose only when every bypass class above has a deterministic, CRLF-stable, semantic fixture proving it can be both rejected and accepted, the same fixtures pass against built output, and release:check fails closed on any mutation or drift. A form that passes only its own naively authored inputs is not hardened.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["contact form", "form=\"contact-form\"", "semantic form validation", "unnamed control rejection", "native submit rejection", "required attribute order", "computed request access", "aliased request access", "console denial", "Resend payload", "second-sink", "CRLF-normalized", "mutation fixture", "release:check", "HTML-aware tag boundary", "comment-safe payload", "template interpolation rejection", "Turnstile resource detection", "passive Turnstile"], "profile": "spec-architecture", "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 1150, "discoveryGroup": "sequence", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P123", "seq": "123", "name": "Gemini YouTube Video / Playlist Ingestion Builder", "type": "BUILD + ARTIFACT", "class": "AI ENGINEERING / YOUTUBE INGESTION", "sprintRole": "Give Gemini a domain-agnostic YouTube source brief that routes knowledge extraction versus ingestion-build missions, binds the immediately preceding source, forces full-source/tail coverage for inspectable content, writes reusable findings when authorized, and produces source-specific documents or standalone ingestion artifacts without pretending repository access", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "You have a YouTube video, Short, playlist, directly attached accessible media, saved yt-dlp JSON, or transcript and want Gemini to extract reusable knowledge without domain bias, build ingestion artifacts, or both; especially when prior output was front-loaded, trailed off later, buried knowledge under implementation scaffolding, or exported generic/URL-named documents.", "inspectFirst": "Resolve the immediately preceding unambiguous source, the user's mission mode, available source extent/timestamps or transcript ordering, supplied ledger/schema and canonical domain vocabulary, export/write surface and authority, then verified donor dossier and actual available tools. Never infer inaccessible repository state.", "expectedOutput": "A mission-routed result: for knowledge extraction, a polished source-specific report with one Finding record per distinct reusable insight, full source coverage/tail receipt, human-readable document title/export basename, and canonical ledger records written with exact mutation receipt when writable/authorized or row-ready records otherwise; for ingestion build, the existing standalone yt-dlp adapter/contracts/fixtures/tests/JSON/CSV packet and repository-capable handoff; for both, knowledge first and implementation second.", "nextStep": "Finish the primary mission at the correct proof layer: close the forward plus reverse/tail coverage ledger, write or emit reusable findings, verify source-specific document/export identity, and only when ingestion build is in scope hand the standalone packet to a repository-capable executor for integration and live acceptance. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "The immediately preceding unambiguous source is bound without restatement; semantic claims are source-grounded; domain classification follows source+mission rather than favorite domains; supplied Sources/Findings/Domains ownership is preserved; when the ledger is writable and write authority exists, mutations have exact written ranges/IDs; inspectable content is COMPLETE only when the available extent including its tail is dispositioned with no unaccounted span, otherwise PARTIAL names the gap; per-source exports have a source/use-case-specific visible title and basename rather than a raw URL or generic repeated title; knowledge-only missions are not buried under implementation scaffolding; yt-dlp remains the metadata extraction authority; existing normalization, spreadsheet-safety, donor-license, proof-ceiling, and repository-access boundaries remain enforced.", "color": "Teal", "copySheet": "P123_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "GEMINI YOUTUBE INGESTION. USE SOURCE; EXTRACT KNOWLEDGE, BUILD ONLY REQUESTED ARTIFACTS, AND DO NOT PRETEND REPOSITORY ACCESS.\n\nConsumer/product: xyz_consumer_product\nTarget repository identity, if one exists: xyz_repo_name_for_handoff_only\nYouTube source: use the immediately preceding accessible YouTube video/link/attachment when unambiguous; otherwise xyz_video_playlist_or_fixture\nVerified donor dossier / pinned source research: xyz_donor_dossier\nConsumer data-model requirements: xyz_consumer_contract\nRequired JSON fields / CSV columns: xyz_output_fields\n\nMISSION\nTurn source + mission + ledger/schema + donor evidence into grounded knowledge and only the artifacts the user's mission calls for. Never let early-source fluency substitute for full-source review.\n\nMISSION MODE / PRIMARY DELIVERABLE ROUTING\nResolve `MISSION_MODE`: `KNOWLEDGE_EXTRACT`, `INGESTION_BUILD`, or `BOTH`.\n- `KNOWLEDGE_EXTRACT`: knowledge report + ledger write/row-ready records are primary. Do not emit adapter/donor/repo-handoff boilerplate unless requested or materially required.\n- `INGESTION_BUILD`: implementation packet is primary; retain source understanding needed by the build.\n- `BOTH`: knowledge report first; implementation packet is secondary.\nNever default every use case to the same document shape.\n\nSOURCE INPUT RESOLUTION\n- A YouTube video, Short, playlist URL, directly attached media item, saved yt-dlp JSON, or supplied transcript/captions is valid input; a single video is valid input.\n- If exactly one usable source appears immediately above this prompt or in the same turn, bind it as `SOURCE_INPUT` automatically. Do not ask the operator to paste, repeat, or restate it into a placeholder.\n- Record `SOURCE_INPUT_KIND` and `SOURCE_INPUT_IDENTITY`. If the video itself is directly inspectable, perform grounded semantic analysis from accessible audiovisual/transcript evidence. If only a URL is present and the environment cannot inspect its content, do not invent what the video says; state the proof ceiling.\n- Source placement is input binding, not repository access.\n\nFULL-SOURCE COVERAGE / TAIL-CHECK CONTRACT\n- Inspect the available source from first through last available position. Run a chronological forward pass, then a deliberate reverse/tail pass.\n- Maintain `COVERAGE_LEDGER`: `span | evidence | disposition | finding_ids`. Disposition every span as NEW_FINDING, SUPPORTS_EXISTING, NO_REUSABLE_INSIGHT, or UNAVAILABLE_UNVERIFIED.\n- With timing/position metadata report `SOURCE_EXTENT`, `LAST_INSPECTED_POSITION`, `UNACCOUNTED_SPANS`, `FULL_SOURCE_COVERAGE`. `COMPLETE` is allowed only when the available extent including the tail is accounted for; otherwise report `PARTIAL` and the exact gap.\n- If context/tool limits require chunking, preserve order/provenance, reconcile duplicates, and never promote a truncated pass to COMPLETE.\n\nDOCUMENT IDENTITY / EXPORT CONTRACT\n- Derive a source/use-case-specific `DOCUMENT_TITLE`; the first visible line/H1 must be that title, not code, `Python`, a raw URL, prompt name, or generic template label When the result may be exported from a Gemini chat into Google Drive, treat that first line/header as the Drive document title and make it source-specific before any other content..\n- Derive a matching safe `EXPORT_BASENAME`; do not use the raw source URL as the document/file name. Add a short source ID/date only when disambiguation is needed.\n- Reusable ledgers may retain canonical names, but each per-source exported report/document must identify its source/use case distinctly.\n- Do not export unresolved `xyz_` placeholders or irrelevant repository scaffolding into a knowledge-only document.\n- Report `DOCUMENT_TITLE`, `EXPORT_BASENAME`, and observed file/Drive ID or URL when an actual export/write occurs.\n\nDOMAIN-AGNOSTIC KNOWLEDGE / LEDGER CONTRACT\n- Classify from source + mission, never recent conversation themes, favorite domains, or examples. Examples prove range, not defaults: Cybersecurity; Agentic Software Development; Culinary & Food.\n- With a supplied ledger/schema, `Sources`, `Findings`, and `Domains` are canonical; reuse its canonical domain vocabulary. favored views are projections, not separate data authorities.\n- Produce one Finding record per distinct reusable insight with provenance/evidence/confidence/validation. Use Unknown / Needs Verification when needed. Set Prompt Kit Candidate / Software Candidate only when applicable.\n- Resolve spreadsheet write capability + authority. If writable and write authority exists, perform the canonical Source/Findings write and report exact written ranges/IDs as the mutation receipt. Otherwise emit row-ready Source and Findings records. Never claim the spreadsheet was updated without an observed write receipt.\n\nGEMINI CAPABILITY BOUNDARY\nAssume the target repository is NOT accessible unless actually exposed. Repository names/excerpts are not access.\n- Use only supplied/accessed source material and verified donor evidence; MUST NOT fabricate a repository patch, imports, paths, schemas, tests, CI, branches, SHAs, merges, or runtime proof.\n- Suggested destinations are `PROPOSED LOCATION — REQUIRES REPO-CAPABLE AGENT TO VERIFY`.\n- Classify repository claims as SUPPLIED_CONTEXT, PROPOSED, or UNKNOWN_REQUIRES_REPO_INSPECTION.\n\nSOURCE AUTHORITY / DONOR CONTRACT\n- yt-dlp owns machine-readable YouTube metadata extraction; consume its JSON, do not reimplement YouTube HTML parsing, and do not create two competing extraction authorities. This does not forbid semantic analysis of video/transcript content directly available in Gemini context.\n- Consumer code owns normalization/schema/tests/exports.\n- TubeArchivist and NewPipeExtractor are reference sources only where license boundaries require it; do not copy GPL implementation code.\n- Record supplied donor pins and runtime `yt-dlp --version`; never invent observations.\n\nWINDOWS-FIRST EXTRACTION CONTRACT\nLive metadata uses external yt-dlp with `--skip-download`, `--dump-single-json`, and `--no-warnings` when suitable, without downloading media and does not request media download. `--flat-playlist` is explicit lower-metadata census mode. Support `--input-json` for deterministic fixtures.\n\nNORMALIZATION + IDENTITY / OCCURRENCE INVARIANTS\nnormalized JSON is canonical; CSV is a projection.\n- A unique source/video entity represents stable identity; a playlist occurrence is ordered membership, references source identity, and must not duplicate the canonical source entity. preserve every observed occurrence.\n- Tracking/share parameters such as `si=` do not create identity. Prefer extractor-supplied `playlist_index`; use encounter-order fallback only when needed and record `position_source`.\n- SOURCE-LIST REGRESSION EXAMPLE: synthetic corpus has 25 URL occurrences and 23 unique video IDs; `_CuibYl_Fh0` and `bBdq2hf5R0I` repeat. A share/tracking parameter such as `si=` must not create a new video identity.\n- UNAVAILABLE / COMPLETENESS CONTRACT: null/private/deleted slots retain occurrence tombstone and must not silently shrink. States: COMPLETE, PARTIAL, EMPTY_CONFIRMED, EMPTY_UNPROVEN, FAILED. Empty usable results require extractor evidence or explicit `--allow-empty`.\n\nJSON + CSV CONTRACT\nPreserve Unicode/commas/quotes/newlines/missing values. Use `utf-8-sig` and verify UTF-8 BOM for spreadsheet-facing CSV unless overridden. Cells beginning with `=`, `+`, `-`, or `@` must be spreadsheet-safe while canonical JSON must remain unchanged.\n\nDONOR EVIDENCE / VERSION CONTRACT\nMissing pins/releases/licenses/observations are NOT_SUPPLIED or UNKNOWN. Preserve ADOPT / ADAPT / REFERENCE_ONLY / REJECT / DEFER; must not silently change a supplied donor disposition. Separate `normalization_schema_version` and `adapter_version`; emit `donor_manifest.json`.\n\nBACKEND-NEUTRAL NORMALIZATION CONTRACT\nRaw extractor responses are backend-local and must not be the shared domain contract. A YouTube Data API adapter must not impersonate yt-dlp JSON. All backends adapt to the canonical schema.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION PACKET — ONLY WHEN MISSION MODE INCLUDES `INGESTION_BUILD`\nFor `INGESTION_BUILD` or `BOTH`, produce standalone applicable files: `source_ingest_youtube.py`, `source_import_contract.json`, `youtube_playlist_fixture.json`, `test_youtube_source_ingestion.py`, `donor_manifest.json`, representative normalized JSON/CSV, compact Windows run sheet. Do not import from hypothetical consumer-repository modules.\n\nOUTPUT PATH SAFETY CONTRACT\nGenerated outputs live under `Outputs/` by default. Resolve paths and reject equal resolved input/output paths; source fixture remains byte-identical after rejection. Any authorized non-Outputs overwrite gets a timestamped backup under `Outputs/backups/`.\n\nRUNNABILITY GATE\nRun deterministic tests when execution exists; else mark UNRUN. Tests must import `subprocess` when used, create/validate output directory, include actual non-ASCII Unicode fixture and embedded quote, exercise formula prefixes, verify BOM, support deterministic timestamp, and fixture-mode CLI that writes both JSON and CSV plus donor manifest.\n\nMINIMUM DETERMINISTIC TESTS\nTest full no-download command; explicit flat mode; normal metadata; repeated video ID preserves multiple ordered occurrences; CSV derives from JSON; Unicode/comma/newline round trip; spreadsheet-safe while JSON remains semantically unchanged; malformed donor case; collision rejection; fixture-mode CLI writes both JSON and CSV deterministically.\n\nLIVE-PROOF CEILING\nFixture tests do NOT prove current YouTube behavior, live metadata/auth/private access, repository compatibility, or integration. Directly inspectable media supports only bounded semantic observations. If live proof runs, record runtime version/source identity/count/outputs/failures without credentials.\n\nREPOSITORY-CAPABLE HANDOFF\nOnly when repository integration is part of `MISSION_MODE`, end with a copy-paste handoff.\nPRE-MUTATION MISSION DECLARATION: declare repository and branch/worktree, lane and mission, owned and forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation order, proof ceiling, mutation authority. Refresh remote truth; read repository governance and current Git/PR state; find the existing source/import/domain owners; enforce one writer per mutation surface. Run focused/repository gates and `git diff --check`; use normal commit and push when authorized. Report changed files, executed checks and results, commit SHA, push/PR state, blockers, Git status, proof ceiling, and the exact next command. Preserve authority: yt-dlp owns YouTube parsing, consumer owns normalization/schema/tests/exports. Do not make the operator restate the donor research.\n\nFINAL RESPONSE\nStart with `DOCUMENT_TITLE`, source identity, `MISSION_MODE`, and full-source coverage receipt. For `KNOWLEDGE_EXTRACT`, return the polished source-specific knowledge report and ledger mutation/row-ready records first and stop when that mission is complete. For `BOTH`, knowledge first, implementation second. For `INGESTION_BUILD`, return applicable packet/proof/handoff. Report `EXPORT_BASENAME` and observed export receipt. The result is incomplete if source coverage silently trails off, document identity is generic, or proof is fabricated.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["gemini youtube prompt", "gemini youtube", "youtube playlist ingestion", "youtube playlist metadata", "youtube metadata scraper", "yt-dlp playlist", "yt-dlp metadata", "youtube json csv", "playlist metadata adapter", "youtube source ingestion", "gemini implementation packet", "youtube donor dossier", "youtube playlist windows", "youtube video ingestion", "single youtube video", "youtube short", "youtube video attachment", "video above prompt", "preceding youtube video", "youtube knowledge extraction", "youtube knowledge base", "domain agnostic youtube", "knowledge ledger", "youtube findings ledger", "full source coverage", "youtube tail coverage", "source specific document title", "document export naming", "knowledge report"], "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 1151, "discoveryGroup": "sequence", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P124", "seq": "124", "name": "Repository Code Readability & Structural Refactorer", "type": "MAINTENANCE + FACTOR", "class": "ENGINEERING / CODE READABILITY", "sprintRole": "Refactor bloated or tangled source code into cohesive, obvious ownership boundaries that humans and agents can understand and edit safely without behavior drift", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "Source code has become difficult to navigate or change because files, functions, classes, modules, branches, responsibilities, naming, duplication, or indirection have grown beyond clear human/agent editability, even when tests still pass. When paradigm selection itself is the primary defect, route that bounded decision/repair to P128 Programming Paradigm Fit & Boundary Refactorer. Also use when the operator names only a bloated or hard-to-edit repository and wants it made more readable; rank concrete hotspots from repository evidence instead of making the operator choose a file.", "inspectFirst": "Repository governance and code map; target source and tests; public APIs, schemas, serialization and artifact contracts; call/import graph and ownership boundaries; generated-code/source ownership; file/function/class size and complexity signals when available; duplication and dead-code evidence; recent change hotspots and regressions; representative maintainer edits and the current owner/change sites they require; review/churn hotspots when repository evidence is available; formatter/linter/type/build/test conventions.", "expectedOutput": "A behavior-preserving readability refactor with a structural-debt ledger and ranked repository hotspot map when scope starts broad, smaller and clearer ownership surfaces, characterization/regression tests where needed, measured or evidence-backed before/after editability improvements, no hand-edited generated output, and exact validation/integration proof.", "nextStep": "When no hotspot is named, rank the strongest evidence-backed repository hotspots and select the smallest top-ranked behavior-preserving slice; otherwise refactor the highest-impact evidenced readability hotspot now, run its characterization/regression and repository gates, perform a deliberate second pass for new indirection or leftover structural debt, then integrate the exact green bounded slice or name the precise blocker. If evidence shows paradigm mismatch rather than general structural debt, hand that boundary to P128 programming-paradigm specialist without broadening P124. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "Externally observable behavior and owned contracts remain unchanged unless explicitly in scope; each moved responsibility has one obvious owner; duplicate/dead/tangled code is removed only with evidence; generated surfaces are changed through their canonical source/generator; the candidate does not replace a monolith with a maze of trivial abstractions; representative maintenance paths are easier to locate and edit; targeted and impacted regressions pass; and a deliberate second pass finds no practical high-impact readability repair remaining in owned scope. Paradigm-specific redesign is either unnecessary or explicitly routed to P128; P124 does not absorb a second architecture doctrine.", "color": "Cyan", "copySheet": "P124_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "REFACTOR THE REPOSITORY CODE FOR HUMAN AND AGENT READABILITY. DO NOT TURN THIS INTO COSMETIC FORMATTING, A REWRITE FOR ITS OWN SAKE, OR A LINE-COUNT CONTEST.\n\nRepo: xyz_repo_or_path\nTarget scope or hotspot: xyz_scope_or_resolve_from_evidence\nKnown readability pain, if any: xyz_readability_problem\nForbidden behavior changes: xyz_forbidden_behavior_or_resolve_from_repo\n\nMISSION\nMake the owned source code easier for a fresh human or agent to navigate, understand, modify, review, and test while preserving externally observable behavior and repository contracts unless a behavior change is explicitly in scope. Treat passing tests as necessary but not sufficient: a green change that needlessly creates a god file, giant function, mixed responsibility, duplicate logic, or an abstraction maze has not solved the editability problem.\n\n1. REFRESH AND MAP THE REAL OWNER\n- Refresh current repository/default-branch truth and overlapping work before mutation. Preserve dirty or separately owned work.\n- Read repository governance, code maps, target source/tests, public APIs, schemas, serializers, artifact contracts, and generated-code ownership.\n- Trace practical callers/imports and identify where a maintainer would currently change each responsibility. Do not refactor from file size alone.\n\n2. BUILD A STRUCTURAL-DEBT LEDGER\nFor each concrete hotspot classify it as KEEP, EXTRACT, SPLIT, RENAME, DEDUPLICATE, DELETE-DEAD, MOVE-TO-CANONICAL-OWNER, GENERATED-SOURCE-REPAIR, or DEFER. Support the disposition with code/test/history/tool evidence. Useful signals include:\n- one file/class/function owning unrelated responsibilities;\n- deep nesting or branch logic that obscures the main path;\n- duplicated conditions, transformations, constants, or error handling;\n- names/comments that hide domain intent or disagree with behavior;\n- broad mutable state, hidden coupling, circular or surprising imports;\n- one small change requiring edits across many unrelated places;\n- repeated agent/human difficulty finding the correct owner;\n- dead compatibility paths or wrappers with no remaining consumer;\n- hand-edited generated output instead of its canonical generator/source.\nLine counts, cyclomatic complexity, churn, fan-in/fan-out, or duplication metrics may prioritize inspection when available, but no universal threshold automatically requires a split.\n\n2A. REPO-WIDE INTAKE + REPRESENTATIVE EDIT\nIf no hotspot is named, do not ask the operator to pick one. Rank 3-5 candidates by mixed responsibility, duplicated change sites, churn/regressions, hidden coupling, and difficulty locating a real edit. Record `rank | path/owner | evidence | representative edit | change sites | risk`. Size is a clue, never a verdict. Pick the smallest high-value slice. Before and after, answer `Where do I change <behavior>?`; success means clearer ownership and fewer ambiguous change sites without new indirection.\n\n3. PROTECT BEHAVIOR BEFORE MOVING IT\n- Identify public/API/CLI/schema/serialization/output/artifact behavior that must survive.\n- Add or strengthen characterization/regression tests before risky movement when current coverage does not pin the behavior.\n- Separate behavior changes from readability refactors. If a semantic repair is necessary, make its contract and proof explicit rather than hiding it inside cleanup.\n- Never weaken tests, validators, types, schemas, or error handling merely to make extraction easier.\n\n4. REFACTOR FOR COHESION, NOT SMALLNESS ALONE\n- Give each module/class/function one coherent reason to change and one obvious owner.\n- Keep related logic together; extract only when the new boundary has a meaningful name, contract, reuse point, test seam, or independent responsibility.\n- Prefer direct control flow and domain-revealing names over cleverness. Flatten avoidable nesting with guard clauses or smaller coherent operations when that clarifies the path.\n- Centralize duplicated deterministic rules in the existing canonical owner rather than inventing another helper family.\n- Keep public surfaces narrow and dependencies explicit.\n- Comments should preserve WHY, invariants, hazards, or non-obvious constraints; remove comments that merely narrate code or became stale.\n- Remove proven dead code and obsolete compatibility shims only after checking callers/history/contracts.\n- If a file is generated or derived, repair its canonical source/template/generator and regenerate it. Never hand-edit generated output for tidiness.\n\n5. DO NOT REPLACE A MONOLITH WITH A MAZE\nReject refactors that create excessive tiny files, wrapper chains, pass-through classes, one-use abstractions, indirection without ownership value, generic utility dumping grounds, or dependency injection ceremony with no demonstrated seam. Local readability and cohesive proximity matter. A large function or module may remain when it is coherent, well-named, and safer to understand in one place.\n\n6. ITERATE ON THE HIGHEST-VALUE HOTSPOT\nPass 1: implement the smallest behavior-preserving structural repair with targeted proof.\nPass 2: read the resulting diff from the perspective of a fresh maintainer and ask: where would I change this responsibility now; did the edit surface become more obvious; did extraction create new indirection, duplication, or scattered ownership; what high-impact structural debt remains? Repair concrete in-scope findings and rerun affected checks. Continue only while a practical evidenced readability gain remains; do not manufacture churn.\n\n7. PROVE EDITABILITY, NOT JUST GREEN TESTS\nRun formatter/linter/type/build/tests and repository validators appropriate to the touched code. Compare before/after structural signals when tools or simple measurements are available, but do not optimize a proxy alone. Include at least one representative maintenance question or edit path such as `Where would a fresh maintainer change <responsibility>?` and prove it resolves to a smaller or more obvious owner set without losing required context. Run `git diff --check` and inspect the final diff for behavior drift and needless movement.\n\nSCOPE BOUNDARY\n- P76 owns harness/spec progressive disclosure; P78 owns documentation diet; P63 owns skill factoring; P68 owns model-context engineering; P06 owns Git/PR cleanup. Use those owners when the primary defect lives there instead of absorbing their scope into this prompt.\n- Do not combine unrelated feature work with readability cleanup merely because nearby code is ugly. If a broader cleanup is useful but not required for the bounded change, record and route it separately.\n\nDELIVER\nReport the structural-debt ledger, highest-impact repairs, files/functions/modules moved or simplified, preserved behavior/contracts, characterization/regression proof, before/after editability evidence, validation, commit/PR/merge/mainline state, remaining deferred debt, and the exact next executable action. Keep the closeout concise; the code should carry the readability improvement.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["code readability", "codebase readability", "code bloat", "source code bloat", "structural refactor", "maintainability refactor", "code cleanup", "god file", "giant function", "giant class", "mixed responsibilities", "duplicate code", "editability", "human readable code", "agent readable code", "repository readability audit", "hard to edit repository", "readability hotspot"], "profile": "spec-architecture", "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 1152, "discoveryGroup": "sequence", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P128", "seq": "128", "name": "Programming Paradigm Fit & Boundary Refactorer", "type": "DESIGN + BUILD", "class": "ENGINEERING / PROGRAMMING PARADIGMS", "sprintRole": "Choose, preserve, or repair programming paradigms at concrete code boundaries so object-oriented, functional, procedural, declarative, data-oriented, event-driven, and hybrid techniques are used deliberately rather than by habit", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "The primary engineering question is whether a source boundary should remain or become object-oriented, functional, procedural/imperative, declarative, data-oriented, event/message-driven, or an explicit hybrid; or when accidental paradigm mixing materially worsens state ownership, side-effect clarity, coupling, testability, or maintainability.", "inspectFirst": "Repository and language/framework conventions; the current responsibility and callers; state, identity, invariants, mutation, side effects, data flow, lifecycle, concurrency/asynchrony, performance evidence, public contracts, tests, and the neighboring P124 structural-readability owner.", "expectedOutput": "A bounded paradigm-fit ledger for materially affected boundaries, an evidence-backed PRESERVE/ADOPT/HYBRIDIZE/DEFER disposition, the smallest behavior-protected implementation needed to repair a real mismatch, focused regression proof, and explicit routing of general structural cleanup back to P124.", "nextStep": "Classify the highest-impact affected boundary by state, effects, ownership, data flow, lifecycle, concurrency, and repository idiom; implement the smallest justified paradigm repair or preserve the current design with evidence; then rerun focused and impacted checks and review the resulting seam once more. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "No paradigm is selected by ideology or fashion; behavior/contracts remain protected; changed boundaries make state, effects, ownership, and execution semantics at least as explicit as before; hybrids have explicit seams; paradigm-specific failure modes are checked; general structural cleanup remains with P124; targeted and impacted tests pass; and a second pass finds no practical in-scope paradigm mismatch remaining. The repository writing-sprint declaration is resolved before mutation, including branch/worktree, lane, owned/forbidden scope, dependencies/collisions, expected artifacts, validation order, integration authority, proof ceiling, and commit/PR expectation.", "color": "Cyan", "copySheet": "P128_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "CHOOSE OR REPAIR THE PROGRAMMING PARADIGM AT THE ACTUAL CODE BOUNDARY. DO NOT FORCE OOP, FUNCTIONAL PROGRAMMING, OR ANY OTHER PARADIGM AS A REPOSITORY-WIDE RELIGION.\n\nRepo: xyz_repo_or_path\nBranch/worktree: xyz_branch_or_worktree_or_resolve_from_repo\nLane/sprint: xyz_lane_or_sprint_name\nOwned scope: xyz_owned_scope\nForbidden scope: xyz_forbidden_scope\nDependencies/collision risks: xyz_dependencies_and_collisions_or_resolve_from_repo\nExpected tracked artifacts: xyz_expected_artifacts_or_resolve_from_repo\nValidation order: xyz_validation_commands_or_resolve_from_repo\nIntegration authority: xyz_merge_or_integration_authority_or_resolve_from_provider\nProof ceiling: xyz_proof_ceiling\nCommit/PR expectation: xyz_commit_pr_expectation_or_resolve_from_repo\nTarget responsibility/boundary: xyz_boundary_or_resolve_from_evidence\nObserved paradigm concern: xyz_concern_or_unknown\nForbidden behavior changes: xyz_forbidden_behavior_or_resolve_from_repo\n\nSPRINT DECLARATION — REQUIRED BEFORE MUTATION\nResolve the declaration above from repository/provider evidence before editing. Do not begin mutation with branch/lane/scope/collision/artifact/validation/integration/proof fields left implicit; involve the user only for a genuinely user-only decision.\n\nMISSION\nMake paradigm choice deliberate and local. Preserve a coherent existing design when it fits. Change it only when evidence shows that another paradigm or explicit hybrid makes ownership, state, side effects, data flow, concurrency, testing, or maintenance materially clearer without unbounded rewrite. General code readability and structural factoring remain owned by P124 Repository Code Readability & Structural Refactorer; this prompt owns the paradigm-fit decision and the bounded repair it requires.\n\n1. PIN THE REAL BOUNDARY\nRefresh repository truth and inspect the responsibility, callers, tests, language/framework idioms, public contracts, state/mutation, effects/I-O, lifecycle, data flow, concurrency/asynchrony, and performance constraints. Do not infer a paradigm from filenames or syntax alone. Identify which boundary is actually hard to reason about or change.\n\n2. BUILD A PARADIGM-FIT LEDGER\nFor each materially affected boundary record CURRENT PARADIGM, EVIDENCE, FAILURE/FRICTION, and one disposition: PRESERVE, ADOPT, HYBRIDIZE, or DEFER. Compare only plausible local choices. A campaign succeeds by correct fit, not by maximizing the number of boundaries converted.\n\n3. FIT RULES — TOOL, NOT RELIGION\n- OBJECT-ORIENTED: prefer when identity, lifecycle, encapsulated mutable state/invariants, polymorphic substitution, or stateful collaboration is the domain story. Reject data-only service objects, deep inheritance, mutable god objects, and class ceremony around stateless transforms. Favor composition over inheritance when substitution is not the real requirement.\n- FUNCTIONAL: prefer for deterministic transforms, rules, validation, calculation, immutable data flow, and pipelines. Keep effects at explicit boundaries. Reject hidden mutable closures, unreadable combinator cleverness, and purity theater around inherently stateful lifecycle/I-O code.\n- PROCEDURAL / IMPERATIVE: prefer when ordered orchestration and I-O sequence are the clearest domain story. A readable linear workflow does not need classes or functional combinators merely to appear sophisticated. Keep mutation scoped and control flow obvious.\n- DECLARATIVE: prefer for configuration, schema/query, UI/state descriptions, routing, policy, or framework-owned execution. Ensure declarations do not conceal imperative lifecycle effects, ordering, or error behavior that maintainers must understand.\n- DATA-ORIENTED: prefer when measured throughput, locality, allocation, serialization, or batch/hot-loop behavior makes data layout the dominant constraint. Require performance evidence before architecture churn.\n- EVENT / MESSAGE-DRIVEN: prefer only for genuine asynchronous/integration boundaries. Make event identity, ordering, idempotency, delivery/retry, failure, and observability semantics explicit. Do not introduce a bus or queue to disguise a local synchronous call.\n- HYBRID: valid when seams are explicit—for example functional core + imperative shell, stateful objects + pure domain helpers, declarative configuration + imperative adapters. Mixing is not a defect by itself; accidental mixing is debt when ownership or effects become ambiguous.\n\n4. PROTECT BEHAVIOR BEFORE CONVERSION\nCharacterize externally observable behavior, contracts, serialization, errors, and important side effects before risky movement. Add the smallest missing regression first. Do not hide semantic feature changes inside paradigm refactoring, weaken tests to ease conversion, or hand-edit generated output.\n\n5. IMPLEMENT THE SMALLEST JUSTIFIED REPAIR\nChange only the boundaries whose ledger disposition requires it. Preserve repository/language idioms unless evidence justifies departure. Keep public surfaces narrow. Prefer explicit adapters at paradigm seams. Do not convert an entire module tree because one function benefits from purity, or create a class hierarchy because one object has state. If the real problem is simply giant/mixed/duplicated source rather than paradigm fit, route it to P124 instead of stretching this prompt.\n\n6. PARADIGM-SPECIFIC FAILURE REVIEW\nAfter the first green result, falsify the chosen design: OOP -> leaked mutable state/inheritance coupling; functional -> hidden effects/closure mutation/order dependence; procedural -> sprawling shared mutation/control tangles; declarative -> hidden execution/order/effect behavior; data-oriented -> unmeasured optimization/semantic leakage; event-driven -> duplicate/out-of-order/lost/retried events; hybrid -> unclear seam or two competing owners. Repair practical findings and rerun affected checks.\n\n7. PROVE FIT, NOT PREFERENCE\nRun formatter/linter/type/build/tests and owning validators. Show why the selected or preserved paradigm fits this boundary using concrete state/effect/ownership/data-flow/concurrency evidence. Perform a second-pass maintainer question: `Where is state owned, where do effects occur, and where would I change this rule now?` The answer should be obvious from a smaller owner set, not dependent on taste.\n\nDELIVER\nReport the paradigm-fit ledger, boundaries preserved/changed, P124-routed structural debt, behavior protection, focused regressions, validation, commit/PR/mainline state, proof ceiling, and exact next action.\n\nCLOSURE RULE\nClose when every materially affected boundary has a supported PRESERVE/ADOPT/HYBRIDIZE/DEFER disposition, required repairs are green, paradigm-specific failure review finds no practical in-scope defect, and no repository-wide conversion was invented merely for stylistic consistency.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["programming paradigm", "functional programming", "object-oriented programming", "OOP", "procedural programming", "imperative programming", "declarative programming", "data-oriented programming", "event-driven programming", "message-driven programming", "functional core imperative shell", "composition over inheritance", "paradigm mismatch", "hybrid programming paradigm"], "profile": "spec-architecture", "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 1156, "discoveryGroup": "sequence", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P129", "seq": "129", "name": "Cross-Input UX Modality & Phone-Native Interaction Architect", "type": "DESIGN + BUILD", "class": "PRODUCT / UX INPUT MODALITY", "sprintRole": "Design and implement equivalent but mode-native interaction languages for mouse/pointer, keyboard power users, and phone/touch users, with phone treated as a first-class workflow rather than a resized desktop skin", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "An app or UX-critical repository supports or should support mouse/pointer, keyboard, and phone/touch users, but one modality is an afterthought; especially when mobile means larger buttons or compressed desktop controls, touch depends on hover/double-click/browser Find, keyboard has a real power language while phone has no equivalent grammar, or equivalent capabilities diverge by input path.", "inspectFirst": "Fresh product/repository truth; primary user jobs and terminal actions; semantic command/action and state owners; pointer controls; keyboard shortcuts/sequences/focus rules; phone controls/gestures/command sheets, safe-area and software-keyboard behavior; responsive/accessibility contracts; browser/device proof; repeated cross-app patterns; overlapping UX/runtime work.", "expectedOutput": "A three-mode capability matrix, mouse/keyboard/phone interaction grammar, semantic-action convergence map, smallest phone-native repair for the highest-value gap, modality failure regressions, browser/device proof with explicit physical-phone ceiling, and routing of proven reusable patterns to P109.", "nextStep": "Audit one high-frequency terminal journey across mouse/pointer, keyboard-only, and phone/touch; repair the first missing, desktop-shaped, or semantically divergent phone capability on the existing action/state owner; then rerun all three modes plus phone-specific falsification before broadening. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "Every in-scope high-value capability has a reachable mode-native route; equivalent pointer, keyboard, and phone entrypoints converge on the same semantic action/state owner; phone does not depend on hover, tiny desktop chrome, double-click, browser Find, or accidental software-keyboard focus; gestures have labeled fallbacks and avoid scroll/browser conflicts; one physical action cannot double-dispatch through touch/pointer synthesis; touch emulation is not promoted to physical-phone ergonomic proof; and a second pass finds no practical in-scope modality gap.", "color": "Cyan", "copySheet": "P129_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "DESIGN THREE REAL UX LANGUAGES: MOUSE DIRECT CONTROLS, KEYBOARD POWER COMMANDS, AND PHONE-NATIVE TOUCH. DO NOT CALL BIGGER BUTTONS A MOBILE EXPERIENCE.\n\nRepo/product: xyz_repo_or_product\nBranch/worktree: xyz_branch_or_worktree_or_resolve_from_repo\nLane/sprint: xyz_lane_or_sprint_name\nOwned scope: xyz_owned_scope\nForbidden scope: xyz_forbidden_scope\nDependencies/collision risks: xyz_dependencies_and_collisions_or_resolve_from_repo\nExpected tracked artifacts: xyz_expected_artifacts_or_resolve_from_repo\nValidation order: xyz_validation_commands_or_resolve_from_repo\nIntegration authority: xyz_merge_or_integration_authority_or_resolve_from_provider\nProof ceiling: xyz_proof_ceiling\nCommit/PR expectation: xyz_commit_pr_expectation_or_resolve_from_repo\nUX surface/journey: xyz_surface_or_resolve_from_context\nObserved phone failure: xyz_phone_failure_or_resolve_from_evidence\n\nSPRINT DECLARATION — REQUIRED BEFORE MUTATION\nResolve the declaration above from repository/provider evidence before editing. Recover existing interaction contracts instead of making the user restate them.\n\nMISSION\nGive mouse, keyboard, and phone users intentional interaction languages over one product model. Mouse gets direct pointer-native controls. Keyboard gets a real power-user command language. Phone gets touch-native journeys, not compressed desktop chrome or bigger buttons. Equivalent intent converges on the same semantic actions, state owners, feedback, and terminal results.\n\nOWNED SCOPE\n- Cross-input capability mapping, mode-native interaction grammar, phone/touch design, semantic-action convergence, bounded modality repair, regressions, and honest device proof.\n\nFORBIDDEN SCOPE\n- Do not replace P106 overall UX architecture, P99 flow/telemetry repair, P109 cross-app factoring, P110 acceptance-only certification, or P95 runtime architecture. Do not create a second mobile state machine or command registry.\n\n1. PIN CAPABILITIES BEFORE CONTROLS\nFor each high-value terminal capability record MOUSE, KEYBOARD, PHONE, semantic action owner, state owner, and proof. Include discover/navigate/search/select/inspect/act/recover/reset/filter/saved-state/error behaviors as applicable. Missing is not equivalent to “use the desktop control.”\n\n2. DESIGN THREE MODE-NATIVE LANGUAGES\nMOUSE / POINTER — visible direct controls, click, double-click, hover, context actions, drag/scroll where natural. Hover may enrich; it may not be the only route to an essential action.\nKEYBOARD — direct shortcuts, command/finder entry, navigation, focus recovery, and short sequences where useful. Prompt Kit’s language includes `/`, `A–E`, `F`, `[`, `]`, `R`, `Home/End`, `Esc`, and prompt sequences; other products must resolve their own bindings rather than cargo-cult these keys. A command should reach terminal value, not merely reveal a mouse control.\nPHONE / TOUCH-NATIVE — tap, structured command sheets/quick controls, conflict-safe gestures, thumb-reachable frequent actions, intentional text-entry focus, safe-area/viewport behavior, and structured destinations. Do not require hover, double-click, tiny desktop chrome, browser Find, or precision pointer behavior. Do not automatically summon the software keyboard when opening a non-text command surface.\n\n3. SHARE SEMANTICS, NOT CONTROL SHAPES\nUse CAPABILITY -> SEMANTIC ACTION -> MODE ADAPTER -> FEEDBACK/STATE. Equivalent click, shortcut, and touch routes call the same canonical action and preserve orthogonal state. Different mode-specific controls are healthy; duplicated business rules, preference truth, selection state, or success semantics are not. Prevent one touch from double-firing through synthesized pointer/click events.\n\n4. MAKE PHONE FEATURE-COMPLETE\nWalk the phone path from entry to terminal value. In Prompt-Kit-like products inspect discover/browse hierarchy, search, Favorites/saved destinations, open/read, terminal action such as copy, back/recovery, reset, filters, and zero-results/error. Mobile simplification is not permission to omit capabilities.\n\n5. GESTURES NEED FALLBACKS AND CONFLICT RULES\nEvery important non-obvious gesture needs a labeled route or command-sheet equivalent. Define cancellation/thresholds so vertical scroll, browser/OS back, text selection, long-press, and tap/click synthesis do not fight the app. Gestures accelerate; they do not become invisible single points of access.\n\n6. HANDLE PHONE-SPECIFIC FOCUS + VIEWPORT FAILURES\nCheck initial focus, software-keyboard invocation, obscured actions, safe areas, orientation/visual-viewport changes, and back/Escape/dismiss restoration. A narrow desktop screenshot does not prove phone usability.\n\n7. IMPLEMENT THE SMALLEST MISSING MODE SEAM\nChoose the highest-value capability with the weakest modality. Reuse the canonical semantic action/state owner and add only the adapter/control surface needed. Route unsound overall journey/IA to P106 and redundant-step/lost-state flow debt to P99.\n\n8. FALSIFY AFTER FIRST GREEN\nMouse: hover-only dependence, hidden direct action, double-click ambiguity.\nKeyboard: collisions, focus loss/traps, intermediate-only completion, sequence ambiguity, Escape erasing unrelated state.\nPhone: accidental software-keyboard opening, action sheet scroll, swipe vs scroll/back conflict, touch/pointer double dispatch, unreachable thumb action, safe-area/orientation breakage, gesture-only capability, long-press/text-selection conflict.\nRepair practical findings and rerun the shared semantic action plus adjacent controls.\n\n9. PROVE EQUIVALENT TERMINAL CAPABILITY\nProve each supported mode reaches the intended terminal result with equivalent state/feedback semantics. Use state tests for convergence, browser automation for focus/geometry/input, and touch emulation for deterministic gesture mechanics. Physical-phone ergonomics, software-keyboard feel, thumb reach, OS/browser gesture conflict, and real touch comfort require an observed phone/device pass before claiming phone acceptance.\n\n10. SECOND PASS + FACTORING\nReverse the journey for hidden desktop assumptions, skipped phone states, duplicated modality logic, missing recovery, and one-mode-only features. When a proven modality pattern repeats across apps, route it to P109 instead of copying adapters.\n\nROUTING BOUNDARY\nUse P106 for overall UX architecture; P99 for flow/state/telemetry refinement; P109 for proven cross-app patterns; P110 for release-grade cross-mode/device acceptance; P95 when semantic command/state ownership itself is unresolved.\n\nDELIVER\nReport the three-mode capability matrix; interaction grammar; semantic convergence; phone-native repair; failures found/repaired; regressions; browser/device evidence; physical-phone proof ceiling; files; integration state; exact next unproven modality action.\n\nCLOSURE RULE\nClose only when every in-scope capability has a mode-native route or explicit supported exclusion, equivalent intent shares canonical semantics/state, phone is a complete interaction language rather than resized desktop chrome, practical modality failures are repaired, and proof claims match observed runtime/device evidence.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["phone native ux", "mobile interaction design", "touch first ux", "cross input ux", "input modality", "mouse keyboard phone ux", "phone interaction language", "mobile not bigger buttons", "touch gestures", "keyboard power user ux", "semantic action convergence", "mobile quick controls", "software keyboard ux", "physical phone acceptance", "one handed ux", "gesture discoverability"], "profile": "spec-architecture", "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 1157, "discoveryGroup": "sequence", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P130", "seq": "130", "name": "Repository Versioning System Establisher", "type": "BUILD + FACTOR", "class": "REPOSITORY / VERSIONING + RELEASE IDENTITY", "sprintRole": "Establish one evidence-based, enforceable repository/product versioning authority, bump policy, synchronized version surfaces, and release identity path before ordinary release execution begins", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "A repository or product has no coherent human-facing release version, has multiple drifting version labels, uses commit hashes or schema versions without an operator-facing release sequence, applies version bumps inconsistently, or needs a durable versioning system before normal merge/release execution can be trusted.", "inspectFirst": "Current default branch and recent tags/releases; package/plugin/app manifests; visible version labels; schemas/protocol versions; changelog/release-note tooling; CI/release workflows; artifact identity contracts; consumers and compatibility promises; rollback/deprecation history; then credible existing release/versioning tooling already present in the stack.", "expectedOutput": "A repository-native versioning authority and policy with justified scheme selection, deterministic bump rules, synchronized product-version surfaces, changelog/tag/release wiring, compatibility/migration/rollback rules, bootstrap/cutover evidence, focused regression proof, and integration into the current default branch; actual publishing remains a downstream gate when release credentials or production authority are separate.", "nextStep": "Implement the selected version authority and its smallest enforceable automation now, prove bump and synchronization behavior with fixtures or dry runs, integrate the exact green change into the current default branch, then hand ordinary release execution to the repository release owner. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "One canonical human-facing product release authority is named; exact Git/artifact identity remains distinct; the scheme choice is evidence-based rather than cargo-culted; bump/no-bump semantics and synchronized surfaces are machine-checkable; migration does not rewrite history or reuse released versions; compatibility, deprecation, rollback, changelog, tag, and release behavior are explicit; focused tests and registered validators pass; and the validated owned change is contained in the refreshed default branch.", "color": "Teal", "copySheet": "P130_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "ESTABLISH AND ENFORCE ONE VERSIONING SYSTEM FOR THIS REPOSITORY. DO NOT MERELY EXPLAIN THE CURRENT LABELS, ADD A DECORATIVE VERSION NUMBER, OR START TAGGING RELEASES WITHOUT FIRST DEFINING THE AUTHORITY THAT MAKES THOSE TAGS MEAN SOMETHING.\n\nRepository / product: xyz_repo_or_product\nCurrent version clues, if known: xyz_tags_manifests_labels_or_unknown\nCompatibility / consumers: xyz_consumers_or_resolve_from_evidence\nRelease target, if any: xyz_release_target_or_not_yet\n\nMISSION\nTurn ambiguous, missing, or fragmented versioning into one durable repository-native system. Establish how a human-facing product release is identified, how the next version is derived from changes, which files and surfaces must agree, how tags, releases, and changelogs are produced, and how compatibility or rollback is handled. Preserve exact commit/artifact identity as proof; a friendly release label supplements exact identity and must never replace it.\n\n1. REFRESH AND INVENTORY THE REAL VERSION SURFACES\n- Refresh the remote default branch and inspect current tags and releases before mutation.\n- Find every version-like surface that can be mistaken for product authority: package/application manifests, plugin metadata, desktop/mobile bundle versions, UI labels, API versions, schema/protocol versions, database migration generations, generated artifacts, docs, release workflows, changelogs, and Git tags.\n- Classify each as PRODUCT RELEASE VERSION, SCHEMA/PROTOCOL VERSION, BUILD/ARTIFACT IDENTITY, DEPENDENCY VERSION, or DESCRIPTIVE DISPLAY ONLY.\n- Do not promote a schema version, capability version, visible vXX label, date, or commit abbreviation into product authority merely because it already exists.\n- Identify current consumers and compatibility promises. If no meaningful compatibility boundary exists, say so instead of pretending SemVer semantics exist.\n\n2. REUSE PRIOR ART WITHOUT CARGO-CULTING IT\n- Inspect release/version tooling already present in the repository and ecosystem before creating a new mechanism.\n- Consider established patterns such as Changesets-style reviewed version PRs and synchronized manifest/plugin versions, release-please or semantic-release style automation, conventional changelog tooling, or a small repository-owned version command when those fit the stack.\n- Adopt mechanics, not slogans. Do not install a heavyweight release framework when a small deterministic owner is sufficient.\n- Preserve one source of truth. Generated or mirrored version fields must derive from or be validated against that owner.\n\n3. CHOOSE THE VERSION SCHEME FROM PRODUCT SEMANTICS\nSelect and justify the smallest scheme that matches the actual product:\n- SemVer when MAJOR/MINOR/PATCH can be tied to a real public compatibility contract.\n- CalVer when time or cadence is the primary release identity and compatibility is governed separately.\n- A monotonic release/build sequence, or another documented scheme, when neither SemVer nor CalVer truthfully models the product.\nDo not choose SemVer merely because it is familiar. Record why the selected scheme fits and what evidence would justify changing schemes later.\n\n4. DEFINE DETERMINISTIC BUMP AND NO-BUMP RULES\nCreate a change-to-version matrix. For SemVer, define what this repository specifically means by breaking/major, compatible feature/minor, and compatible fix/patch. For another scheme, define equivalent advancement rules.\nExplicitly decide how to handle:\n- docs-only, tests-only, refactors, internal tooling, and generated-file-only changes;\n- user-visible fixes and features;\n- breaking API, config, data-format, or CLI behavior;\n- prereleases, release candidates, hotfixes, and backports when relevant.\nThe rule must let two competent executors derive the same next version from the same accepted change set. If human judgment remains, name the narrow judgment gate instead of disguising it as automation.\n\n5. ESTABLISH ONE CANONICAL VERSION AUTHORITY\n- Name the authoritative file, metadata source, or release record: one canonical version authority for the human-facing product release.\n- List every synchronized version surface and whether it is generated, mirrored, or independently schema-versioned.\n- Add a deterministic check that fails when synchronized version surfaces drift.\n- Keep schema/protocol versions and API versions independent when their compatibility lifecycle is not identical to the product release lifecycle.\n- A Git tag must resolve to the exact validated release commit/artifact; a UI badge alone is never freshness proof.\n\n6. BOOTSTRAP WITHOUT REWRITING HISTORY\n- Infer a defensible starting version from existing releases, tags, and product maturity when possible; otherwise choose and document an explicit cutover version.\n- Do not rename old tags, rewrite published history, decrement a version, or reuse a released version number to make history look cleaner.\n- Record the cutover rule so old ad-hoc labels remain interpretable without remaining authoritative.\n\n7. INSTALL THE RELEASE MECHANICS\nImplement the smallest repository-native path that fits the chosen policy:\n- a version/bump or version-PR command;\n- synchronized manifest/update logic where required;\n- changelog or release-note generation from reviewed change evidence;\n- tag/release creation wiring only after owning validation succeeds;\n- CI or hooks that reject impossible versions, version drift, reused tags, or releases from an unvalidated or stale commit.\nWhere release publication needs credentials, protected environments, stores, registries, or production authority, install and prove everything up to that boundary and name the exact downstream gate.\n\n8. COMPATIBILITY, DEPRECATION, MIGRATION, AND ROLLBACK\n- Define what a consumer can infer from a version change.\n- For breaking changes, require migration and deprecation evidence appropriate to the product.\n- Treat rollback as redeploying or restoring a previously identified artifact or commit, not decrementing or reusing a version.\n- If a release must be withdrawn, preserve its historical identity and document replacement or yank behavior rather than erasing it.\n\n9. PROVE THE SYSTEM BEFORE CALLING IT DONE\nAdd focused tests, fixtures, or dry-run proofs that cover at least:\n- representative no-bump and bump cases;\n- the highest-impact compatibility or breaking case for the chosen scheme;\n- synchronization of all declared product-version mirrors;\n- idempotent version calculation from the same accepted change set;\n- tag/release identity pointing at the exact validated commit/artifact;\n- failure when a version surface drifts or a released version would be reused.\nRun the repository's owning validators and builds plus git diff checks. Do not claim a published/runtime release merely from static or CI policy proof.\n\n10. CONVERGE, THEN RELEASE THROUGH THE DOWNSTREAM OWNER\nIntegrate the exact validated versioning-system change into the refreshed default branch when authorized. Report the canonical version authority, current or cutover version, scheme and bump matrix, synchronized surfaces, automation entrypoint, changelog/tag path, compatibility and rollback rules, validation evidence, and proof ceiling.\nOnce the system is established, ordinary merge/release execution is downstream work. Reuse the repository's existing release executor rather than turning this prompt into a perpetual release prompt.\n\nDEFINITION OF DONE\nThe repository has one human-friendly release-version authority whose semantics are deterministic and enforced; exact Git/artifact identity remains available for forensic freshness; competing version-like surfaces are classified instead of conflated; release mechanics cannot silently drift mirrors or reuse history; compatibility and rollback meaning is documented; representative bump behavior is tested; and the validated system is integrated into the current default branch or stopped at one exact external publication or authority blocker.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/proof, and completion gate. If several actions remain, order them by dependency and keep executing agent-capable steps inside the active authority instead of merely listing them.\n- If continuation will cross into another agent/chat and useful work remains, append HANDOFF as one self-contained copy-paste continuation containing the canonical source/repo, exact branch/PR/SHA or artifact identity, proven floor, remaining gap/blocker/successor obligation, forbidden scope, and first executable action. Do not add a ceremonial handoff when no continuation remains.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only when every material requested-scope item has a supported disposition, no SAFE & EXECUTABLE item remains in the active prompt/lane, the owned acceptance criteria are proven, authorized integration is complete or explicitly blocked, and any REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK is durably preserved with an executable handoff rather than mislabeled as OUT OF SCOPE.\n\nOPERATOR STATE PRESENTATION CONTRACT\n- Treat status icons/emoji as a deterministic rendering layer over typed evidence state, never as machine authority or a second workflow enum. The typed state text remains authoritative and MUST appear beside any icon.\n- Canonical rendering families: ✅ PROVEN / VALIDATED / INTEGRATED / DONE; 🟡 IN_PROGRESS / PARTIAL; 🔵 READY / SAFE & EXECUTABLE; ⏳ WAITING; ⛔ BLOCKED; ⚠️ RISK / UNPROVEN / DEGRADED; 🟣 REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK; ⚪ HISTORICAL / NOT APPLICABLE / RETIRED; ❓ UNKNOWN. Preserve the strongest typed state actually proven; rendering must never promote evidence.\n- When at least three related phases, contracts, lanes, or work items have differing material states, lead the relevant orientation/closeout with a compact status matrix using `Item | State | Meaning | Exact next transition`. For one item, a compact inline state is sufficient.\n- When one gate dominates the program, surface the critical path before narrative as `⛔ CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when blocked or `🔵 NEXT CRITICAL PATH: <gate>` when dependency-ready.\n- Do not hand-author icon literals independently across prompts, make color/icon the only state carrier, or create a separate status machine merely for presentation. Consumers such as plans, ledgers, P02 orientation, P143 convergence, reports, CLI, and future Factory Work Graph/UI should reuse the same typed-state presentation contract.\n\n\nCOMPUTE AUTHORITY / SCOPE-BOUNDARY CONTRACT\n- A bounded sprint limits mutation ownership and blast radius; it does not imply minimal reasoning, one attempt, one test case, or the earliest passing stop.\n- When the operator or execution environment grants broad compute/iteration authority, use as much safe available compute as is materially useful inside authorized surfaces: repeated evidence passes, variants, falsification, diagnostics, instrumentation, controlled retries, cross-checks, and independent validation. Prefer useful evidence volume over token-conserving minimalism.\n- Treat the first PASS as a checkpoint, not an automatic stop signal. Continue while another practical safe, progress-bearing experiment can materially reduce uncertainty, falsify the current conclusion, cover an acceptance condition, or close an evidence gap. Stop at an evidence-backed fixed point or exact blocker, not an arbitrary attempt count.\n- Bounded waits/retries mean each wait, retry, mutation, and external action is bounded and observable; they do not impose a one-pass ceiling on the overall investigation.\n- Broad compute authority never expands safety or mutation authority: it does not authorize unbounded spend, destructive or personal-data mutation, secret exposure, unrelated feature work, bypassing required approval, monopolizing shared resources, or crossing a forbidden scope.\n- If deeper proof requires another canonical owner, invoke or route that owner when safely executable and then return to the current proof/acceptance gate. Routing is not terminal merely because the current bounded slice passed.\n\nEXHAUSTIVE AVAILABLE COMPUTE RULE\n- Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default for Prompt Kit work unless the operator, governing contract, provider quota/cost boundary, safety rule, or execution environment imposes a tighter ceiling. Do not ask for permission to use more reasoning, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots that are already available inside that ceiling.\n- Exhaust the decision-relevant safe compute available before stopping. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context windows, retries, variants, worker slots, or validation passes on the operator's behalf while another practical progress-bearing pass can close a required contract, test or falsify a live hypothesis, cover an acceptance criterion, reduce material uncertainty, expose a regression, or strengthen proof.\n- Exhaust the useful work surface, not arbitrary churn: sweep the acceptance criteria, end-state contract horizon, relevant hypothesis/failure space, affected protected controls, available evidence sources, and applicable proof surfaces. A fixed point is evidence-defined, not attempt-count-defined.\n- Examples, minimum pass counts, bounded retries, or an earlier successful check are floors/controls, not ceilings. Do not stop merely because a stated minimum number of passes succeeded or because the first green result looks plausible.\n- Order compute economically without minimizing it: run cheap/focused checks early to find defects quickly, then broader, adversarial, integration, and environment-dependent checks when they can materially change confidence or close a contract.\n- When at least two meaningful lanes are independent, their writes do not conflict, and at least two usable worker/tool slots exist, dispatch them immediately and execute them concurrently. `Considered parallelism`, `could parallelize`, lane enumeration, or deferred parallelism is not execution evidence. Rejoin through one coordinator, synthesize the returned evidence, and rerun affected combined validation. If usable parallel capacity is absent, continue serially rather than inventing workers; state the exact capability ceiling only when it affects proof or closeout.\n- Before declaring fixed point, perform one explicit residual-compute sweep: identify any still-unspent safe action that could materially improve correctness, coverage, evidence, integration, deployment, runtime observation, or operator acceptance. Execute every such action that is authorized and dependency-ready; otherwise classify it BLOCKED, UNSAFE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, OUT OF SCOPE, or redundant with evidence.\n- Exhaustive compute never expands mutation, safety, privacy, spending, credential, approval, or ownership authority. It increases depth inside the authorized envelope; it does not widen that envelope.\n\nNON-PROGRESS / QUIESCENCE CONTRACT\n- Safe and executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation. Remaining work must also be progress-bearing.\n- Progress-bearing work must materially advance an acceptance criterion, proof state, implementation state, unresolved hypothesis, blocker, or required continuation artifact. Status narration, citation refresh, or rewriting the same blocker is not progress.\n- Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate evidence. Revalidate only when a proof-relevant implementation, contract, schema, dependency, launcher, validator, environment assumption, or target changed.\n- PROOF-RELEVANCE FINGERPRINT: represent the proof inputs as a canonical ordered set of identity/revision pairs for the behavioral implementation, governing contracts and schemas, direct dependencies, launcher and validator, target/environment assumptions, and acceptance/proof inputs actually used. Persist that set in the proof, receipt, or ledger when a durable evidence owner exists; otherwise include it in the evidence-bearing closeout. Compare exact canonical entries: any added, removed, or changed entry invalidates the affected proof; repository HEAD movement with an unchanged set does not. If the fingerprint cannot be reconstructed or compared, freshness is UNKNOWN and the affected proof must be rerun or fail closed.\n- A documentation, ledger, citation, timestamp, or proof-SHA-only mutation MUST NOT trigger another runtime proof merely because that mutation changed repository HEAD. Never create a commit or PR whose only purpose is to replace an otherwise-valid proof citation with the newest repository tip.\n- When the decisive remaining gate requires an unavailable environment, credential, physical machine, user action, review, or external event, persist the blocker once and enter a quiescent BLOCKED state. Do not manufacture repository mutations to remain active.\n- Repeating the same blocker against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint is not a new evidence pass. Two consecutive materially identical blocker observations require a non-progress review before further mutation; if no new behavioral hypothesis or changed proof-relevant input exists, stop.\n- A worker must not create work merely to satisfy a continuation rule. Exhaustive compute applies to useful hypothesis and evidence space, not bookkeeping churn.\n\nEND-STATE CONTRACT HORIZON\n- Before terminal closeout, identify the contracts that define the whole requested outcome, including contracts whose canonical owner or proof surface sits outside the bounded sprint that just ran. Local mutation authority limits what this lane may change; it does not erase downstream obligations from the completion picture.\n- At minimum consider each applicable contract family: implementation/behavior; regression/protected behavior; validation/evidence; integration/default-branch; deployment/environment; live runtime; operator/user acceptance; safety/privacy/data; durability/automation/observability; documentation/runbook/handoff.\n- For every applicable contract, report: CONTRACT; canonical OWNER; STATUS as PROVEN, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, BLOCKED, UNPROVEN/UNKNOWN, NOT APPLICABLE, or OUT OF SCOPE; strongest EVIDENCE; required NEXT TRANSITION; and the exact ACTION/GATE that closes or advances it. Never use `OUT OF CURRENT SCOPE` or `OUT OF CURRENT DESIGN SCOPE`: those phrases confuse a phase/lane boundary with the whole-outcome boundary.\n- The contract horizon is broader than mutation authority. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary limits mutation ownership only; it does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Do not modify unrelated or separately owned surfaces merely because they appear in the horizon, but do not call the whole outcome complete while a required contract remains unproven.\n- Distinguish `LOCAL PROMPT CONTRACT CLOSED` from `WHOLE OUTCOME CONTRACT CLOSED`. A local pass may close the active prompt only when any still-required downstream contract is either BLOCKED with its gate or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK with a durable owner/handoff; deployment, runtime, acceptance, durability, or another required contract remains open until independently proven.\n- If a required contract outside the active prompt/lane is safe and executable by an authorized canonical owner available in the environment, continue into that successor owner when the governing prompt permits. If the prompt deliberately ends at a design/prototype/build boundary, classify the still-required contract as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and hand it off; if an external/user-only gate blocks it, classify it BLOCKED.\n- Include a compact `CONTRACT HORIZON` table or equivalent structured block in the final response whenever more than one material completion contract applies.\n\nISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT\n- Apply this contract whenever work mutates version-controlled repository state or dispatches concurrent mutating writers; read-only work does not require a writer lane.\n- Before writing, refresh the default branch/provider floor and inspect current/open/recent pull requests, branches, worktrees, commits, and expected changed files for ownership collision. For every declared required integrated slice or dependency, prove `git merge-base --is-ancestor <required-sha> <refreshed-default>` and then prove its governing contract/content is still materially present using current content plus its owning validator; ancestry alone cannot prove current behavior after a revert. Any failed ancestry, content, or owning-validator check requires reconciliation and fresh proof before lane creation, mutation, or integration. Reuse the canonical owner when safe. Otherwise give each independent writer one isolated lane pinned to the refreshed base: prefer a Git worktree + dedicated branch; when worktrees are unavailable, use an environment-provided isolated clone/workspace pinned to the same base. Never reuse or modify another writer's branch/worktree/workspace.\n- Isolation is not merely a branch name. Keep each writer's filesystem and dependency environment separate when the toolchain requires it. Git worktrees do not automatically isolate shared ports, databases, caches, lockfiles, environment files, credentials, services, or generated shared outputs; allocate separate resources or serialize those collisions.\n- Each writer commits only its owned scope. The first finisher may advance its own integration gate, but must not reset, clean, delete, repurpose, overwrite, or merge-away unfinished sibling work. Preserve every sibling lane and its unique commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, and handoff state.\n- CONVERGE LOCALLY BEFORE REMOTE DEFAULT when local Git is available: use a dedicated convergence worktree/branch based on the refreshed default, integrate completed lanes in dependency order, resolve collisions explicitly, keep unfinished lanes untouched, and rerun the combined owning validators/build/tests on the integrated candidate. Before integration, refresh the default again and repeat required-slice ancestry plus current-content/owning-validator proof whenever the floor moved or a proof-relevant dependency changed. Update the local default only when repository policy permits and the candidate is proven; then push/open/merge through the normal remote gates. Do not mutate a shared default checkout while independent writers are active unless governing repository policy explicitly requires a singular default-branch writer.\n- In a provider-only or remote execution environment where local Git/worktrees are unavailable, an isolated remote branch/PR is the fallback. State that local convergence is unavailable, validate the exact remote candidate, and never promote provider proof into local-main/workstation proof.\n- Cleanup is last. Remove a worktree/workspace or delete a task branch only after its integration/closure state is known and a preservation check proves no unique unmerged commits, untracked artifacts, evidence, or operator-owned work would be lost. Prune stale worktree metadata only after that check.\n\nREGRESSION SAFETY / RECURRING DEFECT CONTRACT\n- Treat validator, local required-check, hosted-CI, review, runtime, operator-feedback, retrospective, and commit-history failures as defect incidents rather than disposable cleanup. Repair the immediate instance, then classify its defect family.\n- When the same defect family has two or more independently evidenced occurrences, treat recurrence as a system defect. Repeating the local cleanup or editing one prompt is incomplete when a shared owner exists.\n- Find and strengthen the smallest canonical prevention owner: shared policy, generator, validator, hook, required-check profile, schema, or test floor. P13 owns recurring-process hardening; P94 owns regression design and live-behavior preservation; P100 may contribute transcript judgment incidents; P79 remains the Prompt Kit identity gate.\n- Every systemic repair requires a reproducing negative fixture or mutation case plus a positive control, and the focused semantic regression must be registered in the deterministic floor when repository convention applies.\n- For repository mutation, run patch hygiene before commit and integration: `git diff --check`; staged candidate `git diff --cached --check`; exact candidate `git diff --check <base>...<head>` or the repository-owned equivalent. A recurring whitespace repair that only strips the latest whitespace is incomplete.\n- Prefer repository-owned local required-check profiles and exact-candidate receipts before hosted provider checks when local Git is available. GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, and similar systems are execution adapters or independent proof, not the portable semantic owner of the command/profile contract.\n- Provider unavailability does not erase valid repository-defined local proof, but do not invent hostless merge authority when the active repository promotion contract still requires provider mutation, approval, protected runtime, or another external gate.\n- The retrospective matrix is one intake source, not the defect universe. Local validators, required checks, CI, review, runtime observation, operator feedback, and commit history can create or strengthen a regression family without any prompt-use matrix row.\n- Close the systemic defect only after the instance is repaired, the negative case proves sensitivity, the positive control passes, the shared owner is strengthened, affected local required checks pass when executable, and integration retains the regression. A later recurrence means the prevention mechanism itself needs strengthening.\n\n\nEXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT\n- Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation/proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as execution state, not private operational scratchpad.\n- Govern materiality, checkpointing, publication, recovery, persistence, and hard-termination supervision through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json`; classify incidents through `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`.\n- For every material event, checkpoint the last proven state and emit: `BOUNDARY | IMPACT | PROVED | RECOVERY | NEXT`. Do this even when recovery succeeds immediately; recovery does not erase the boundary or disclosure obligation.\n- Before retrying a mutation whose side effects may be partial or unknown, read back authoritative state and reconcile what actually applied. Never replay blindly.\n- Redact unsafe/private/secret raw detail rather than suppressing the event. The operator still gets the safe operational abstraction, consequence, proof checkpoint, recovery disposition, and next action.\n- Continue safe progress-bearing recovery after publication. A boundary update is not permission to switch from execution to advice or to terminate early.\n- When a relevant writable repository exists, publish repository-relevant or systemic learning through the existing canonical contract, ledger, receipt, tracked plan, negative fixture, or positive control; do not create a second authority or dump private chat into public source.\n- Route recurring/systemic defects through P13 and `harness/contracts/prompt-regression-safety.v1.json`; route retained negative/positive regression design through P94. Repairing the instance without strengthening the prevention owner is incomplete once the systemic threshold is met.\n- Silence is never a terminal state. If the process itself is hard-terminated before it can report, an external supervisor must synthesize the terminal boundary from the last durable checkpoint; agent-local prompts cannot claim this external proof.\n\n- UNKNOWN BOUNDARY FALLBACK: if a material execution transition does not safely fit an existing canonical class, classify it as `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`; checkpoint and publish it normally, then open taxonomy/prevention review. Missing vocabulary is never permission for silence or a guessed specific class.\n- BOUNDARY-TO-SPRINT CONTINUATION: classification is routing, not sprint eligibility. Every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, bind the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, then execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination. Classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal while safe movement remains.\n- ARBITRARY-BOUNDARY RULE: an agent-created scope edge, implementation inconvenience, capability assumption, phase/lane label, or newly noticed boundary may change the recovery mechanics, but it does not silently reduce the requested outcome. Turn it into the next bounded recovery sprint or prove the exact blocker/safety gate that prevents movement.\n- DUAL-LANE SPRINT: the primary boundary sprint applies to every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on unfinished work. Only the separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant events additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical repository owner and regression loop, concurrently when safe or as durable successor work when write surfaces collide.\n- DELIVERY / OUTBOX: a planned update is not a delivered update. Preserve stable event identity and the last proven checkpoint across retries; where the host supports a durable boundary journal/outbox, persist the pending publication before delivery and acknowledge user/repository delivery separately so process loss cannot erase the transition.\n\nFINAL DISPOSITION AUTHORIZATION GUARD\n- FINAL AUTHORIZATION: resolve and obey the active explicit mode/disposition before acting. Every shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery, exhaustive-compute, or successor-work clause above is conditional on that authorization boundary; position, repetition, or later generic `must continue` wording does not expand it.\n- A non-execution disposition MUST NOT start a new implementation, integration, merge, deploy, boundary-recovery execution, exhaustive-compute execution, or successor-execution slice. Mode-authorized read-only analysis and CLOSEOUT checkpoint/handoff durability remain allowed where that mode explicitly permits them.\n- For P02, resolve AUTO before executing; ORIENT and SUMMARIZE remain non-implementation; CLOSEOUT is limited to its mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff durability and must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.", "keywords": ["versioning system", "repository versioning", "product version", "semantic versioning", "semver", "calver", "version authority", "version bump", "release identity", "release tags", "changelog", "changesets", "release automation", "version drift", "compatibility version", "version policy"], "profile": "spec-architecture", "actionabilityPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "boundaryContinuationPolicy": "boundary-to-sprint-continuation/v1", "dispositionAuthorizationPolicy": "actionable-next-command/v1", "discoveryRank": 1158, "discoveryGroup": "sequence", "displayOrderPolicy": "prompt-kit-guided-discovery-order"}, {"id": "P137", "seq": "137", "name": "Evidence-Backed Project Case Study & Demo Deck Builder", "type": "BUILD + ARTIFACT", "class": "PROJECT DEVELOPMENT / PORTFOLIO ANALYTICS", "sprintRole": "Turn a real software, operations, or project-management project into a portfolio-grade demo presentation and case study that reconstructs how it developed, evaluates supported delivery outcomes, quantifies work patterns, compares system-design alternatives, and models evidence-labeled future process scenarios without inventing project facts.", "progress": "YES", "useWhen": "You want to turn a real repository or PM project into a demo presentation, portfolio case study, retrospective, or data-analysis story showing the inspiration, first steps, decision quality and outcomes, lessons, repeatable method, scale requirements, architecture alternatives, time spent, actual task/output distributions, and plausible future distributions under explicit process changes.", "inspectFirst": "The project's current repository/source workspace and governance; exact branch/ref when repository-backed; mutation authority, owned/forbidden scope, protected source-evidence boundary, and canonical Outputs/ location; README/specs/plans; git history, PRs/issues/releases when accessible; project-management artifacts, task/evidence ledgers, time records, decisions, milestones, demos/screenshots and measured outcomes; current architecture/system boundaries; audience/privacy constraints; and any existing canonical artifact or presentation contract. Distinguish direct evidence from inference before calculating, charting, or mutating artifact files.", "expectedOutput": "An actual demo deck and companion case study when artifact tooling is available, plus a reusable evidence table and chart/scenario dataset written under the declared Outputs/ boundary: project origin and evolution, supported delivery/outcome evaluation, lessons and repeatable method, scale/expansion needs, current design and credible alternatives, time-spent and task/output distribution visuals, and baseline-versus-scenario perspective charts whose assumptions and deltas are explicit. Every material metric is labeled measured, derived, estimated, or counterfactual and traces to evidence or a stated model; source evidence remains preserved.", "nextStep": "Declare repository/ref, mutation authority, owned/forbidden scope, protected source evidence, and Outputs/ target; recover the project chronology and quantitative evidence; build the baseline evidence table; then create the narrative, outcome evaluation, actual-distribution charts, system-design alternatives, and counterfactual scenarios. Render the presentation/case-study artifacts, inspect them for factual and visual integrity, and revise until the evidence, calculations, artifact boundaries, and story agree. Do not leave the next step empty or generic: identify the first executable, dependency-aware action that advances or consumes the canonical artifact or proof; PR, status, branch, or log inspection alone is invalid when safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains. Respect explicit mode/disposition authority: generic continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-execution obligations apply only when the active disposition authorizes them; they MUST NOT promote an ORIENT/SUMMARIZE/CLOSEOUT-style disposition into implementation, although a closeout may persist its mode-authorized checkpoint or handoff. Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh and reconcile remote/default-branch truth first; a remembered command or path from an older head is not safe operator guidance. Before claiming completion, reconstruct the original requested/owned scope and disposition every material item as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK. Any SAFE & EXECUTABLE item that is progress-bearing disproves `none; no safe actionable work remains` and must be advanced; merely runnable bookkeeping does not. UNSAFE requires the exact action, concrete hazard and evidence, consequence, and safer alternative or gate; missing tools, credentials, approval, or access is BLOCKED rather than unsafe, and uncertainty or inconvenience alone is not danger. OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. If work is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK, preserve it in durable plan/handoff state, and name its owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate. If the current head is the validated head, or intervening head movement is proven proof-irrelevant by an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, and the owned branch or pull request is mergeable, all required checks and owning harness validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review, conflict, branch-protection, or required-approval gate remains, the acting agent has merge authority, and the user has not prohibited merge, merge it into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method; do not stop at PR-open or green-status reporting unless a named external gate actually blocks integration. Before stopping, emit a compact evidence-bearing closeout: what is completed/proven, changed surfaces and produced artifacts, commands/examples actually verified when applicable, remaining gaps/risks/blockers, unproven runtime or field steps, review/reconciliation state when relevant, proof ceiling, integration state, and the first executable continuation. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation rather than narrating tool use or repeating the plan. After an integration SHA has been proven, a later default-branch advance is not a failure by itself: verify that the proven merge or commit is an ancestor of the refreshed default branch and rerun only proof affected by intervening changes; do not require the refreshed default-branch head to equal the historical integration SHA. When conversation-derived work becomes reusable or execution-relevant, materialize the distilled safe truth into the smallest existing canonical artifact at the next evidence-changing checkpoint instead of leaving it chat-only; do not dump transcripts or create duplicate authorities. When repository planning produces a materially actionable plan, persist the complete plan to tracked repository/provider state before treating planning as complete or handing execution to another agent; chat text is provisional rather than canonical repository state. If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, the approved plan must be present there directly or by an explicit canonical tracked-plan reference. An unrelated or separately owned pull request is evidence to inspect, not a write target; do not modify another lane to satisfy plan durability. Do not promote evidence states: a plan, design, schema, config entry, test, branch, PR, or mention in another chat is not proof that behavior is wired, implemented, validated, integrated, deployed, or observed. Name the strongest state actually proven by refreshed repository/provider/runtime evidence. A bounded sprint limits mutation scope, not useful compute volume: when broad compute/iteration authority is granted, continue safe evidence-reducing passes to a fixed point rather than stopping at the first pass. Before terminal closeout, emit the end-state contract horizon for the whole requested outcome, including required contracts owned outside the current slice without treating that visibility as mutation authority. Treat exhaustive available compute as authorized by default unless an explicit operator, governing-contract, provider-quota/cost, safety, or environment ceiling says otherwise. Do not conserve tokens, tool calls, context, retries, variants, validation passes, or worker slots on the operator's behalf while decision-relevant safe work remains. Exhaust the useful acceptance/contract/hypothesis/proof surface, dispatch independent lanes concurrently when usable capacity exists, and stop only at an evidence-defined fixed point or exact ceiling/blocker—not at an arbitrary pass count or first green. Safe/executable is necessary but not sufficient for continuation: the next action must be progress-bearing. Repository HEAD movement alone does not invalidate proof when proof-relevant inputs are unchanged. If the same external blocker recurs against an unchanged proof-relevance fingerprint, persist it once and quiesce rather than creating citation-only, ledger-only, or bookkeeping work. For repository-mutating work with concurrent or separately owned writers, apply the ISOLATED WRITER / CONVERGENCE CONTRACT: isolate each writer, preserve siblings, prove the combined local convergence candidate when local Git is available, and clean up only after preservation proof. EXECUTION BOUNDARY ACCOUNTABILITY CONTRACT: Treat every material blocker, boundary, capability loss, provider/tool limit, validation or proof failure, safety/privacy gate, partial or uncertain mutation, user interruption, and agent-chosen cessation as first-class execution state rather than private operational scratchpad. Apply `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-enforcement.v1.json` and `harness/contracts/execution-boundary-taxonomy.v1.json`: checkpoint the last proven state, classify the boundary, reconcile possible side effects before retry, immediately publish BOUNDARY / IMPACT / PROVED / RECOVERY / NEXT when material, and continue safe progress-bearing recovery. Successful recovery never erases the disclosure obligation. Redact unsafe raw detail rather than hiding the existence or operational consequence of the event. When a relevant writable repository exists, persist repository-relevant or systemic boundary learning through the existing canonical contract/ledger/receipt/test/plan owner instead of chat-only state or a second authority; recurrence flows through P13 and regression design through P94. If the acting process cannot self-report because it is hard-terminated, only an external supervisor may synthesize that terminal event from the last durable checkpoint. If a MATERIAL or CRITICAL execution change cannot be mapped to an existing class without guessing, use `UE_UNCLASSIFIED_MATERIAL_BOUNDARY`, disclose it normally, and open taxonomy/prevention review rather than keeping it private. Classification is routing, not sprint eligibility: every MATERIAL or CRITICAL boundary on an unfinished requested outcome opens a bounded primary recovery sprint, including already-classified, unclassified, externally caused, and agent-created or arbitrary boundaries. Preserve the original outcome, name the smallest owned recovery scope, first executable action, completion gate, and return condition, and execute that first safe progress-bearing action before ordinary termination; classification, reporting, planning, or handoff prose alone is nonterminal. The separate systemic-prevention lane is conditional: novel/unclassified, recurrent/systemic, or missing-prevention-invariant boundaries additionally open a bounded prevention sprint through the canonical owner without replacing primary recovery. Publication is not considered safely delivered merely because it was planned: preserve a stable event identity/checkpoint so retries are idempotent and host supervision can recover an unacknowledged transition.", "proofGate": "The delivered case study and presentation are grounded in accessible project evidence; repository/ref and mutation authority plus owned/forbidden scope are declared; generated artifacts stay under the declared Outputs/ boundary; source evidence is preserved; any authorized generated-artifact overwrite has a timestamped backup and mapping; inspiration through repeatable-method narrative is complete; outcome-quality claims are tied to supported criteria; time and distribution totals reconcile to their stated denominators; every pie chart sums to 100% within rounding; actual and counterfactual charts are visually and semantically separated; scenario changes state assumptions and conserve/reallocate a defined baseline rather than manufacturing hours or outcomes; system-design alternatives include tradeoffs; sensitive/private data is excluded from shareable artifacts; and generated artifacts are opened/rendered or otherwise inspected when the environment supports it.", "color": "Cyan", "copySheet": "P137_COPY_SAFE", "category": "standard", "copyContent": "CREATE THE PROJECT DEVELOPMENT DEMO PRESENTATION AND CASE STUDY NOW. DO NOT STOP AT AN OUTLINE WHEN THE ENVIRONMENT CAN PRODUCE THE ARTIFACTS.\n\nPROJECT / REPOSITORY\n[repo, workspace, project name, or resolve from current context]\n\nAUDIENCE / PURPOSE\n[portfolio, interview, stakeholder demo, PM case study, data-analysis case study, internal retrospective, or resolve from context]\n\nSPRINT / ARTIFACT BOUNDARY\nBefore creating or changing artifacts, declare:\n- REPOSITORY / WORKSPACE — canonical project/repository location;\n- BRANCH / REF — exact branch/ref when repository-backed, otherwise N/A;\n- MUTATION AUTHORITY — what this run may create or overwrite;\n- OWNED SCOPE — presentation, case-study, evidence-table, chart-data, and scenario-data outputs for this request;\n- FORBIDDEN SCOPE — source evidence, unrelated project files, secrets/private inputs, and any separately owned work.\n\nTreat source evidence as read-only by default. Never overwrite, rename, move, delete, or reformat source evidence merely to produce the case study. Write generated presentation, case-study, evidence-table, chart-data, and scenario-data artifacts under `Outputs/`; if repository governance mandates a different generated-artifact root, place an `Outputs/` subdirectory under that canonical root rather than inventing another location. Create the directory when permitted.\n\nBefore any explicitly authorized overwrite of an existing generated artifact, create a timestamped backup under `Outputs/backups/YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS/` and record the original -> backup -> replacement mapping. Never use this overwrite path for source evidence. If mutation authority, output ownership, or the protected source-evidence boundary cannot be established, stop before file mutation and report the exact blocker.\n\nMISSION\nTurn this real project into an evidence-backed story of how it came to exist, how it was built or delivered, how well supported decisions/outcomes performed, what the work teaches, and how a stronger future version of the process could change effort and output distributions. Produce a demo presentation and companion case study useful for software projects, operational tooling, and PM repositories alike. AxTask, ASB, Triage, and PM repositories are representative use cases, not special-case logic.\n\nEVIDENCE FLOOR BEFORE STORYTELLING\nRecover the strongest accessible project truth before writing the narrative: repository history, commits/PRs/issues/releases, plans/specs, task or project ledgers, time records, milestones, architecture, artifacts, screenshots/demos, decision records, measured outcomes, and relevant current-state docs. Prefer canonical current evidence over memory or polished retrospective prose. Do not invent dates, hours, impact, adoption, savings, team size, performance, task counts, or outcomes to make the story look better.\n\nMaintain an evidence table for material claims with: claim/metric, value, unit/denominator, evidence source, evidence date/version, derivation if any, and evidence class. Use exactly these evidence classes where applicable: MEASURED, DERIVED, ESTIMATED, COUNTERFACTUAL. A DERIVED value shows its formula. An ESTIMATED value states the estimation basis/range. COUNTERFACTUAL values describe modeled futures, never historical fact.\n\nCASE-STUDY STORY ARC\n1. INSPIRATION / ORIGIN — What problem, frustration, opportunity, observation, or real-world need started the project? Separate documented inspiration from later interpretation.\n2. FIRST STEPS — What was the smallest first useful move, prototype, workflow, repository shape, spreadsheet, script, manual process, or architecture? Show why that step made sense with what was known then.\n3. EVOLUTION / DECISION POINTS — Reconstruct important pivots, regressions, discoveries, constraints, rejected approaches, and decisions. Tie claims to evidence instead of creating a heroic linear story.\n4. DELIVERY / OUTCOME QUALITY — Evaluate how well the project or important decisions performed against supported success criteria: correctness, usefulness, cycle time, reliability, adoption, rework, stakeholder outcome, maintainability, or project-specific measures. If no defensible criterion exists, say so rather than inventing a score. Separate outcome from attribution: evidence that an outcome occurred does not prove one decision caused it.\n5. WHAT I LEARNED — Extract technical, product, project-management, data, workflow, and human lessons that the evidence supports. Distinguish a local lesson from a generally reusable principle.\n6. HOW I WOULD DO IT AGAIN — Convert lessons into a repeatable sequence: what to preserve, move earlier, automate, measure, parallelize, simplify, or stop doing. Make the future method executable rather than motivational.\n7. SCALE AND EXPANSION — Explain what would actually be needed to support more users, larger data, more projects, stronger reliability, broader teams, production operations, or richer features. Separate demonstrated bottlenecks from anticipated ones.\n8. SYSTEM-DESIGN ALTERNATIVES — Show the current design plus credible alternatives. For each, compare boundaries, data/state flow, deployment/operations, complexity, cost/effort, failure modes, scalability, and migration consequences. Do not declare an alternative superior without an explicit decision criterion.\n\nTIME-SPENT MODEL\nBuild the best-supported time model available. Prefer direct time records. When only dated activity or repository evidence exists, do not convert commits/messages into precise hours without a defensible estimation method. Show coverage: what period and work are included, what is missing, and whether the denominator is measured or estimated.\n\nAt minimum, prepare a baseline time/work table suitable for visualization. Use meaningful project categories such as discovery/design, implementation, testing/validation, deployment/integration, documentation/communication, operations/rework, or project-specific equivalents. Categories must be mutually understandable and their values must reconcile to the stated total.\n\nACTUAL DISTRIBUTION PIE CHARTS\nInclude useful pie/donut charts only when the parts genuinely form one whole. At minimum include:\n- time/effort distribution by meaningful work category when supported;\n- task or output distribution by meaningful deliverable/workstream category when supported.\nEvery chart must name its denominator, period/scope, evidence class, and unknown/unclassified share if material. Percentages sum to 100% within rounding. Do not use a pie chart for unrelated measures or hide missing data by renormalizing silently.\n\nPERSPECTIVE / COUNTERFACTUAL PIE CHARTS\nCreate a reusable scenario mechanism for intelligent future-process decisions rather than decorative alternative charts.\n1. Freeze one BASELINE VECTOR from the actual evidence: categories, values/shares, denominator, evidence class, and uncertainty.\n2. Define each INTERVENTION explicitly, such as more upfront design, automated tests, reusable scaffolding, better requirements, parallel independent work, stronger observability, reduced manual deployment, an architecture change, or another project-specific change supported by the retrospective.\n3. State the CAUSAL ASSUMPTION: which categories the intervention is expected to increase/decrease and why. Do not present correlation or preference as proven causality.\n4. Produce a SCENARIO VECTOR using one transparent model: reallocate a fixed total, change the total with an explicit assumption, or model a bounded range. Never silently create or destroy hours/tasks.\n5. Validate the vector: nonnegative values; shares reconcile; pie shares sum to 100% within rounding.\n6. Visualize BASELINE vs SCENARIO side by side using the same category definitions where comparison is intended.\n7. Add a DELTA TABLE with absolute/percentage-point change, expected benefit, tradeoff, confidence, and what future observation would confirm or falsify the assumption.\n\nEmit a reusable scenario-data table/sidecar with at least: scenario, category, baseline value/share, scenario value/share, unit/denominator, delta, evidence class, assumption/intervention identifier, and confidence. Keep the chart generation derived from this data so another project or revised assumption can regenerate the perspectives without manually redrawing the pies.\n\nCreate only decision-relevant scenarios. Two or three strong perspectives are better than many arbitrary ones. When uncertainty is high, use scenario ranges/sensitivity rather than false decimal precision.\n\nDATA-ANALYSIS PORTFOLIO VALUE\nUse the project as a real-life data point. Show how raw evidence became a dataset, how categories/denominators were chosen, what transformations were applied, what the charts can and cannot establish, and how the analysis changes a future decision. The case study should demonstrate analytical judgment, not merely chart production.\n\nPRESENTATION / DEMO ARTIFACT\nBuild a coherent visual deck appropriate to the audience. Favor a clear sequence such as: problem/inspiration -> first useful version -> evolution -> current demo/system -> evidence and time distribution -> outcome quality -> lessons -> repeatable method -> scale needs -> architecture alternatives -> future-process scenarios -> recommended next experiment. Use screenshots/diagrams where they clarify the real system. Include speaker notes or a concise talk track when supported by the artifact format.\n\nCreate a companion written case study that can stand without the deck. Keep an evidence/provenance appendix or sidecar rich enough to audit the quantitative claims, while the outward presentation stays readable. Remove secrets, private operational details, client-sensitive data, personal data, and internal-only evidence from shareable outputs; anonymize or aggregate where needed without changing the underlying meaning.\n\nARTIFACT AND VISUAL PROOF\nWhen presentation/document/chart tooling is available, create the actual files, render/open them, inspect chart labels/legibility/layout, verify that quantitative callouts match the evidence table, and repair visible defects. If a required artifact tool is genuinely unavailable, produce the strongest executable content/data package the environment supports and name that exact capability ceiling; do not pretend an outline is a finished deck.\n\nDELIVER\nReturn the presentation and case-study artifact paths/links, evidence-table and scenario/chart-data artifact, a compact list of evidence classes/coverage limits, the baseline distribution, scenario assumptions and deltas, delivery/outcome evaluation, system-design alternatives, and the true proof ceiling. The finished story must answer: what inspired this, how it began, what changed, how well it performed against supported criteria, what I learned, how I would do it again, what scaling requires, what alternatives exist, how the work was actually distributed, and how deliberate process changes might plausibly shift that distribution next time.\n\nACTIONABLE NEXT COMMAND AND NEXT STEPS CONTRACT\n- Do not leave NEXT COMMAND, NEXT ACTION, NEXT STEP, or NEXT STEPS blank, placeholder-only, optional-only, observation-only, or as generic future advice.\n- Advance the work into the next useful unproven state. Start with the first executable action, name its owner and dependency, and state the artifact or proof it will produce.\n- When safe, executable, progress-bearing work remains, do not stop at opening or reopening a PR, displaying already-reported status, listing branches or commits, showing logs, repeating a path, asking the operator to continue, or telling the operator to wait. Those may support diagnosis but are not sufficient next actions.\n- For unmerged remote work, provide one copy-paste command that sets the repository location, fetches without force, verifies the exact branch and commit, preserves dirty or separately owned work through an isolated worktree or another non-destructive mechanism, runs the owning validator, build, or launcher, resolves the canonical artifact from tracked registry, manifest, builder, workflow, or operator documentation, opens or prints that artifact, and propagates every nonzero exit code. After that proof, advance the next available integration gate rather than stopping at inspection.\n- When no artifact exists yet, the next action must create or repair the next owned artifact and prove it; do not substitute inspection for implementation.\n- When review, merge, credentials, a protected runtime, a technician workstation, or production access is the real blocker, name that exact blocker and provide the command or operator action that advances that gate without claiming higher proof.\n- A NEXT STEPS list must be ordered, dependency-aware, owner-assigned, executable, and specific. Generic items such as test, review, merge, deploy, document, or monitor are invalid without the exact command or action, target, expected artifact, and completion gate.\n- Use `none; no safe actionable work remains` only after all authorized work is complete, validation has actually run, commit and push or PR state are reported, cleanup or preserved-work state is reported, and no safe, executable, progress-bearing unproven action remains in the active prompt/lane. Required successor work must still be preserved and handed off explicitly.\n\nDISPOSITION / MODE PRECEDENCE CONTRACT\n- An explicit active mode or disposition is an authorization boundary. Shared execution, continuation, integration, merge, deploy, and successor-work rules may strengthen how authorized execution proceeds, but they MUST NOT promote a non-execution disposition into implementation.\n- Resolve the active disposition before applying generic continuation language. If the selected disposition limits work to orientation, summary, review, or closeout, obey that narrower contract even when safe executable work exists elsewhere.\n- For P02 specifically, ORIENT and SUMMARIZE do not authorize repository implementation; CLOSEOUT may persist the mode-authorized checkpoint/handoff but must not start a new implementation slice; CONTINUE is the execution-bearing disposition that activates Sections 6-8 and the shared execution contracts.\n- A later shared suffix does not override an earlier explicit disposition boundary merely because it appears later in the composed prompt.\n\nEXECUTION BRIEF / SOURCE / DONE / SELF-CHECK CONTRACT\n- ROLE: Operate as the senior practitioner and execution owner for the domain named by this prompt. Use that role to choose relevant standards, checks, and failure boundaries; never invent authority, credentials, access, or facts.\n- WHERE TO LOOK: Start with explicit source, repository, context, plan, artifact, or path inputs in the prompt, then resolve the current canonical owners, governing contracts, tests/validators, and evidence those inputs point to. Do not ask the user to restate evidence the agent can inspect.\n- DEFINITION OF DONE: Before mutation, translate the mission, owned scope, explicit user constraints, acceptance requirements, and proof requirements into a compact observable done checklist. Output existence alone is not completion; every material criterion needs evidence or an explicit UNKNOWN/blocker.\n- SELF-CHECK: Before any completion claim, re-read the current source evidence and verify every material factual or quantitative claim, path, file/artifact identity, SHA/version, validator/test result, and completion statement against the evidence that supports it. Flag unsupported or stale claims instead of filling gaps from memory.\n\nCONVERSATION-TO-ARTIFACT CONTINUITY CONTRACT\n- Treat chat as working memory, not the sole durable owner, once a conversation-derived result becomes execution-relevant or reusable. Durability triggers include accepted or rejected decisions that constrain later work; prototype code/config/schema; examples and fixtures; discovered invariants or owner maps; plans/manifests; validation or proof receipts; unresolved blocker/next-action state; and operator corrections that future work must preserve.\n- Materialize throughout the work at evidence-changing checkpoints, not only at final closeout. Reuse the smallest existing canonical owner—source, spec, contract, schema, test, fixture, manifest, ledger, pull request, report, document, spreadsheet, database/provider record, or established workspace artifact—before creating a new file or provider object. The durable artifact should let another competent agent continue without reconstructing the originating chat.\n- Persist distilled task-relevant truth, not a raw transcript. Never copy secrets, private content, irrelevant personal context, hidden reasoning, or transient brainstorming into repository artifacts merely to create persistence. Keep tentative exploration in chat until it crosses a durability trigger.\n- Bind durable state to provenance and freshness: record enough source/evidence/revision/owner/proof state to distinguish accepted truth from proposal and to detect stale divergence. Before relying on the artifact later, reconcile it against stronger current repository/provider/runtime authority; artifactization must reduce drift, not fossilize stale truth.\n- When the canonical durable home is a connected provider rather than Git, reuse the existing provider identity/workspace and its specialized synchronization owner when one exists; do not create a parallel workspace or reimplement sync/conflict mechanics inside an unrelated prompt.\n- For prototypes, prefer executable seams plus focused tests, fixtures, traces, or receipts over prose-only descriptions. When prototype evidence changes the accepted design, ownership, invariant, or interface, update the existing canonical design/contract artifact in the same pass.\n- If mutation is forbidden or no safe durable owner is writable, classify durability as BLOCKED and preserve the exact safe payload, intended canonical target, responsible owner, and next action in the allowed handoff surface; do not claim durable synchronization.\n- Avoid artifact theater: do not create duplicate summaries, second sources of truth, citation-only commits, or artifacts with no consumer. A durable artifact must govern behavior, prove it, route continuation, or preserve a material decision.\n\nREPOSITORY PLAN DURABILITY CONTRACT\n- When repository planning produces a materially actionable roadmap, sprint map, architecture plan, migration plan, phase plan, implementation sequence, or other execution dependency, the complete accepted plan MUST live in durable repository/provider state; chat alone is not a canonical planning surface.\n- Chat may carry a provisional sketch, critique, or orientation note. Before the plan becomes an execution dependency, is called approved/ready, or is handed to another agent, persist the complete plan in the existing canonical tracked plan/spec/handoff path or active pull request. Reuse an existing plan owner before creating another plan file. If no canonical plan owner or active pull request exists, create the smallest tracked plan artifact that follows the repository existing plan/docs/harness convention, make that path the canonical owner for this work, and then carry or reference it in the PR when one is opened.\n- If a relevant active pull request is the canonical owner for this work and this lane has mutation authority, approval must be reflected there immediately: include the complete plan or the exact committed canonical plan path/revision. If the active PR is unrelated, separately owned, or not writable by this lane, do not modify it; use/create an owned tracked plan/handoff artifact instead and reference the foreign PR only as evidence/dependency. If the task is explicitly read-only or repository mutation is forbidden, do not violate that boundary: keep the chat plan PROVISIONAL, name durable-plan synchronization as BLOCKED by the explicit scope/authority gate, and do not claim the planning state durable or complete. A plan approved in chat triggers synchronization when authorized, not closure by itself.\n- Multi-phase repository work must persist the whole phase map, not only the current phase: completed floor, successor phases, dependencies, owned/forbidden scope, expected artifacts, validation/proof gates, proof ceiling, and deferred work.\n- When P66 or another repository work ledger exists, use it as the continuity index that points to the canonical plan/PR, current proof, owner, and next action; do not replace the complete plan with a terse ledger row or duplicate implementation specification.\n- If a materially actionable plan changes, update the durable plan/PR before handoff or completion. Later agents must be able to recover it from refreshed repository/provider truth without the originating chat.\n- Closeout is invalid when an actionable repository plan, approved revision, or successor-phase map exists only in chat. Persist it first, then report its canonical path/PR and exact revision.\n\nEVIDENCE STATE / NO PROMOTION CONTRACT\n- Keep repository progress claims typed. Distinguish PLANNED/DESIGNED, TRACKED, IMPLEMENTED, WIRED/REACHABLE, VALIDATED, INTEGRATED, DEPLOYED, and OBSERVED when those states matter.\n- Evidence for a weaker state never silently proves a stronger one. Design/config does not prove implementation; implementation does not prove wiring; a test/branch/PR does not prove mainline integration; integration does not prove deployment; deployment does not prove observed runtime behavior.\n- A different chat, agent, worktree, branch, or PR is evidence to inspect, not completion evidence for this thread. Promote state only after refreshed repository/provider/runtime truth resolves the exact artifact, commit, integration, deployment, or observation that proves it.\n- Do not infer that a named future phase was executed because design ingredients, schema fields, tests, branches, or historical plans exist. Report the strongest proven state and the missing transition explicitly.\n- Before terminal closeout, compare the requested target state with the strongest proven state. If the missing transition is SAFE & EXECUTABLE, continue; if it is explicitly delegated by the prompt to a successor implementation owner, classify it as REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK and preserve the handoff; otherwise name the exact blocker. Never manufacture completion by relabeling partial evidence.\n\nREMOTE FRESHNESS / BRANCH FLOOR CONTRACT\n- Before emitting, executing, or asking the operator to run any repository-backed command, launcher, script, test, build, deploy, or file-relative snippet, refresh remote/provider truth first and reconcile the local checkout. If the command itself is the continuation artifact, make its first operations resolve the canonical repository, run `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider equivalent), resolve the actual remote default branch, preserve dirty or separately owned work, fast-forward a clean behind-only checkout with `git pull --ff-only`, and verify the intended command/launcher still exists at the refreshed head before invoking it. A remembered path or command from an older head is not safe operator guidance.\n- Before branch-sensitive conclusions or repository mutation, refresh remote refs with `git fetch --all --prune --tags` (or provider-equivalent remote refresh), resolve the actual remote default branch, and compare the current/tracking head with refreshed remote truth.\n- Inspect current/open/recent overlapping branches and PRs after the refresh. Do not treat an unfetched local branch list, remembered SHA, or stale feature base as the repository floor.\n- If remote/default-branch truth moves while the agent is preparing guidance or work, invalidate the prior floor before emitting the command or creating another lane. Re-inspect overlapping work and reuse/reconcile the current implementation when it already owns the fix; do not create a duplicate repair from stale evidence.\n- Establish a FRESH EVIDENCE FLOOR before BUILD, REPAIR, ARTIFACT, implementation, or certification work: inspect latest relevant commits; owning contracts/specs/profiles/registries/generators; current validators/tests/CI conclusions; registered artifact manifests/reports; and relevant handoff/ledger evidence. Prefer current repository/provider truth over remembered context, prior-agent completion claims, or stale local outputs.\n- For BUILD / REPAIR / ARTIFACT prompts, resolve the current canonical generator/template/schema and latest accepted/reference artifact identity after refresh. Reuse prior artifacts or proof only when their commit, inputs, dependencies, and owning contract still match the current floor.\n- Treat evidence as versioned proof: a successful validator, CI run, build, or live-cert receipt proves the exact head/artifact/input floor it observed. If that head, required base/dependency, generator/profile/schema, or target artifact moves, refresh and rerun the affected proof instead of carrying stale evidence forward.\n- Fast-forward a clean behind-only tracking branch with `git pull --ff-only` or repository-approved equivalent. If diverged or dirty, preserve unique/separately owned work and reconcile with repo policy or an isolated worktree; never force-reset merely to become current.\n- Reconcile an owned feature branch with the latest required default/dependency floor before implementation/proof when that floor materially affects the work.\n- Re-fetch before final exact-head validation/integration. If the base or owned head moved after proof, reconcile and rerun affected checks before merge.\n- Post-integration verification is containment-based, not head-equality-based: after refreshing the default branch, accept a later head when `git merge-base --is-ancestor <proven-integration-sha> <refreshed-default-head>` succeeds and relevant changed-surface validation remains current. Do not fail merely because the default branch advanced after the recorded merge.\n- If the proven integration SHA is not an ancestor of the refreshed default head, treat prior integration proof as stale or invalid, reconcile the discrepancy, and rerun affected proof before claiming completion.\n\nGREEN BRANCH INTEGRATION CONTRACT\n- Treat integration into current `main` or the repository's configured default branch as the normal completion state for repository work, not an optional follow-up. A bounded implementation scope limits what may be changed; it does not require validated owned work to remain stranded on a branch.\n- A feature branch, worktree, or pull request is a temporary execution/review lane. Do not create a new one when a suitable existing owner can be safely reused, repaired, updated, retargeted, integrated, or extended.\n- After implementation and validation, inspect the exact current branch/PR head, required checks, owning harness validators, focused validators, dependency state, blocking reviews, conflicts, and branch-protection requirements.\n- When the exact validated head is mergeable, required checks and owning validators are green, dependencies are satisfied, no blocking review/conflict/protection gate remains, merge authority exists, and the user has not explicitly prohibited merge, merge the branch or PR into the current default branch in the same run using a repository-accepted merge method. Do not merely report that it is green.\n- If several green branches are dependency-related, integrate them in dependency order, refresh/revalidate affected downstream heads after the base moves, and continue merging while the gates remain green.\n- Do not merge when a required gate is pending or failing, the branch head moved after validation and proof-relevant inputs changed or proof relevance cannot be established, an unresolved dependency/review/conflict/protection rule remains, authorization is absent, or the merge would carry unrelated, unreviewed, unsafe, private, secret, or forbidden-scope work.\n- After merge, fetch/resolve the current default branch, verify it contains the intended change with provider merge state plus the owning test/validator/build/content proof, report its resulting SHA, and preserve or close superseded work safely.\n- An open PR, pushed feature branch, or green status is not a valid terminal state while safe authorized integration remains available. If integration is blocked, name the exact gate and advance that gate instead of leaving generic future advice.\n\nP07 MAINLINE CONVERGENCE OVERRIDE\n- For `P07` / `Repo Sprint Executor`, this integration contract is part of the prompt's completion definition, not optional follow-up guidance. Any earlier legacy sentence such as `Do the repo work. Commit it. Then stop.` is superseded by this section: stopping is allowed only after the exact validated owned change is integrated into the current default branch and that branch is verified, or after a named merge exception is proven.\n- P07 must not create a feature branch merely because repository mutation is requested. Reuse the current safe owner when possible; create an isolated branch/worktree only when repository policy, protection, dirty/separately owned work, review, or collision isolation requires it.\n- In P07, branch creation, worktree creation, a commit, a push, an open pull request, review-ready state, or green CI are intermediate evidence only. None is sufficient completion while safe authorized integration remains available.\n- P07's final report must state the integration target, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and the owning verification that proves the default branch contains the intended result. If unmerged, it must state the exact blocking gate and the action that advances that gate.\n\nOPERATIONAL CLOSEOUT / GAP-RISK CONTRACT\n- Before any legitimate stop, report these fields explicitly: COMPLETED / PROVEN; REMAINING GAPS; RISKS; BLOCKERS; PROOF CEILING; INTEGRATION STATE; NEXT ACTION / NEXT STEPS.\n- EVIDENCE-BEARING CLOSEOUT: treat the final report as a compact evidence packet, not a narration of tool use. Spend words on decisions, evidence, uncertainty, and continuation. Required sections may be one line when empty, but use `none — <evidence or reason>` rather than silently omitting them.\n- Under COMPLETED / PROVEN, name the applicable CHANGED SURFACES / ARTIFACTS and the strongest current evidence for each: files/help/code changed; commands or examples actually verified; generated artifacts, screenshots, logs, receipts, or reports produced; validation/checks observed; and commit/PR/merge/mainline identity. Do not list a command under COMMANDS / EXAMPLES VERIFIED unless it actually ran or its execution is independently evidenced.\n- Separate UNPROVEN RUNTIME / FIELD STEPS from ordinary remaining implementation gaps. Repository/static/CI proof must not silently become browser/device/production/operator acceptance. Put inaccessible live proof under PROOF CEILING and name the exact observation or operator gate still required.\n- When review comments, CI findings, failed checks, or an earlier design materially changed the work, include REVIEW / RECONCILIATION: finding -> repair/disposition -> rerun evidence. A stale or superseded finding may be closed with current evidence; it may not disappear silently.\n- For repository work, INTEGRATION STATE should include the target branch, pre/post default-branch SHA when available, PR/merge state, and containment/content proof that the intended change is on the refreshed default branch.\n- Reconstruct the original request, owned scope, explicit constraints, acceptance criteria, and proof requirements before declaring scope exhausted. Disposition every material item exactly once as PROVEN DONE, SAFE & EXECUTABLE, BLOCKED, UNSAFE, OUT OF SCOPE, or REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK.\n- SAFE & EXECUTABLE means progress-bearing work remains inside the active prompt/lane authority and the agent must continue. A merely runnable bookkeeping action is not SAFE & EXECUTABLE for continuation. It is incompatible with `none; no safe actionable work remains`.\n- REQUIRED SUCCESSOR WORK means the item is still required for the whole requested outcome but this prompt explicitly delegates execution to a named successor phase, lane, prompt, or canonical owner. A phase, sprint, lane, prompt, or agent boundary does not make required whole-outcome work OUT OF SCOPE. Record the successor owner, dependency, first executable action, and completion gate, plus the expected artifact or proof when applicable.\n- BLOCKED means the item remains required but cannot advance because of a named dependency, permission, credential, review, protected runtime, user-only decision, or unavailable external system. Missing access is not evidence that the work is unsafe.\n- UNSAFE is a narrow evidence-bearing classification. Name the exact action that would be unsafe, the concrete hazard, supporting evidence, likely consequence, and the safer alternative or gate. Do not relabel uncertainty, inconvenience, time cost, lack of tools, missing credentials, or ordinary review requirements as danger.\n- OUT OF SCOPE requires an explicit scope boundary from the request, governing contract, or forbidden scope that excludes the item from the whole requested outcome. Do not move unfinished requested work out of scope merely to close the task. A phase/lane boundary alone never qualifies.\n- A gap, risk, blocker, or successor obligation must name the affected scope, evidence, consequence, and the action that reduces or closes it. Do not hide known uncertainty behind `looks good`, `green`, `ready`, or an empty section.\n- NEXT ACTION must be the first executable continuation, with owner, dependency, exact command or operator action, expected artifact/pro